const COMBINED_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"ml-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You need an agent to call an external service that has no prebuilt connector, and you want a no-code wrapper that can be reused across many agents in your organization. Which integration pattern best fits?","options":["A Send HTTP request node in each topic","A custom connector","A Bot Framework skill written in C#","A hard-coded value in a variable"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Integration patterns","sub":null,"explanation":"A custom connector is a no-code/low-code wrapper for a REST API that can be created once and reused across many agents, with built-in parsing and error handling. An HTTP request must be reconfigured in each place and can't be shared like a connector.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"ml-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement correctly describes how agent flows differ from standard Power Automate cloud flows?","options":["Agent flows require a separate Power Automate per-user license","Agent flows can't use premium connectors","Agent flows consume Copilot credits based on usage and support human-in-the-loop actions","Agent flows cannot be monitored separately"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Agent flows vs cloud flows","sub":null,"explanation":"Agent flows are billed in Copilot Studio based on usage (no Power Automate license required), can use premium connectors, support human-in-the-loop actions, and their activity can be monitored separately.","source":{"title":"Agent flows in Microsoft Copilot Studio FAQ","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-faqs"}},{"id":"ml-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You build an internal HR agent and want users in Microsoft Teams to be identified automatically without a sign-in prompt. Which publish authentication option should you choose?","options":["No authentication","Authenticate with Microsoft","Authenticate manually with a custom OAuth2 provider","Disable publishing"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Publish channels","sub":null,"explanation":"The 'Authenticate with Microsoft' option automatically sets up Microsoft Entra ID authentication for Teams, Power Apps, and Microsoft 365 Copilot with no manual configuration; in Teams the user is already identified, so they aren't prompted to sign in.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"ml-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After you make changes to an agent that is connected to several channels, what must you do for customers to see the latest content?","options":["Publish the agent again, which applies to all connected channels","Re-add each channel one at a time","Nothing; changes are live immediately","Create a new agent and migrate the topics"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Channels and deployment","sub":null,"explanation":"Publishing applies to all channels associated with the agent. If you change an agent but don't publish, customers continue to interact with the older content across every connected channel.","source":{"title":"Key concepts - Publish and deploy your agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"ml-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a key consequence of selecting the 'No authentication' option for an agent?","options":["The agent can still use tools with the signed-in user's credentials","Anyone who has the link can chat with the agent, and it can't use tools with user credentials","The agent is automatically restricted to your tenant","The agent can only be published to Microsoft Teams"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"No-authentication risk","sub":null,"explanation":"With No authentication, anyone with the link can interact with the agent, and the agent can't use tools that rely on end-user credentials. Microsoft recommends applying authentication for organizational or user-specific agents.","source":{"title":"Key concepts - Publish and deploy your agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"ml-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement reflects how Copilot Studio handles responsible AI and customer data for generative features?","options":["Tenant data is used to continuously train the foundation models","Generative features run on Microsoft Foundry, ground and validate responses, and don't contribute tenant data to model training","Harmful-content moderation must be configured manually by the maker","Responses are never grounded or validated"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Responsible AI","sub":null,"explanation":"All generative AI features in Copilot Studio run on Microsoft Foundry under Microsoft's Responsible AI standards: they ground responses where possible, validate them to reduce incorrect information, moderate harmful content, and don't use tenant data to train the models.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"ml-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"To use an agent flow as a tool that an agent can call, which trigger and response action must the flow contain?","options":["A scheduled trigger and a 'Send an email' action","The 'When an agent calls the flow' trigger and the 'Respond to the agent' action","A 'When a record is created' trigger and a 'Terminate' action","Any trigger, as long as the flow is in the default solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Agent flow requirements","sub":null,"explanation":"To work as a tool with an agent, a flow must be a solution flow that contains the 'When an agent calls the flow' trigger and the 'Respond to the agent' action, and it must respond in real time (synchronously).","source":{"title":"Modify an existing flow to use with an agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flow-modify-use-with-agent"}},{"id":"ml-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"An agent flow contains logic that takes longer than the synchronous response limit. How should you design it so the agent still gets a timely reply?","options":["Set the Respond to the agent action to asynchronous mode","Place long-running actions after the Respond to the agent action so they continue after the response is returned","Split the flow into a desktop flow","Increase the connector response size limit"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Respond to the agent limits","sub":null,"explanation":"The flow must respond within the 100-second action limit. Actions that need to run longer can be placed after the 'Respond to the agent' action; they continue to run (up to the flow run duration limit) after the agent receives its response.","source":{"title":"Modify an existing flow to use with an agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flow-modify-use-with-agent"}},{"id":"ml-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which category of agent flow action requires human intervention, such as approval requests or providing information?","options":["Built-in tools (loops and branching)","AI capabilities","Human in the loop","Connectors"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Human-in-the-loop actions","sub":null,"explanation":"Agent flows include several action types. 'Human in the loop' actions require human intervention—for example, approval requests or requests for information—pausing automation until a person responds.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"ml-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When designing an agent that uses agent flows, which design responsibility falls to the developer?","options":["Designing an error-handling pattern so the agent knows how to handle exceptions","Manually billing each run to Power Automate","Writing the orchestrator's system prompt","Disabling concurrency to guarantee ordering"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Agent flow design considerations","sub":null,"explanation":"Among the design points for agent flows, the developer must design an error-handling pattern so the agent knows how to handle exceptions; performance is also constrained by the API limits of included services and the flow response has a size limit.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"ml-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You want a topic to show an Adaptive Card that collects input and submits a response back to the agent. Which requirement applies to that card?","options":["The card must be added to a Message node only","The card must contain at least one submit button, as it must be interactive","The card can only contain plain text","The card must use schema version 2.0"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Adaptive Card node","sub":null,"explanation":"An interactive Adaptive Card node must contain at least one submit button so the user can submit information back to the agent; Copilot Studio creates output variables from the card inputs. Non-interactive, display-only cards belong in a Message or Question node.","source":{"title":"Ask with Adaptive Cards","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-ask-with-adaptive-card"}},{"id":"ml-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How do you include dynamic, variable-driven content on an Adaptive Card in a Copilot Studio topic?","options":["Switch the Adaptive Card node to a Power Fx formula that references your variables","Hard-code each value in the JSON literal","Use a separate cloud flow for every value","Adaptive Cards can't display variable content"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Dynamic adaptive cards with Power Fx","sub":null,"explanation":"In the Adaptive Card node properties you can switch from JSON to a Power Fx formula, which lets you reference topic or agent variables to insert dynamic content into the card.","source":{"title":"Ask with Adaptive Cards","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-ask-with-adaptive-card"}},{"id":"ml-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which HTTP methods can the Send HTTP request node in a Copilot Studio topic use to call an external REST API?","options":["GET only","GET and POST only","GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, and DELETE","Only methods exposed by a custom connector"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Send HTTP request node","sub":null,"explanation":"The Send HTTP request node supports GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, and DELETE. You can add headers (for example, an Authorization bearer token), supply a body, and parse the response into a Power Fx variable using sample JSON.","source":{"title":"Make HTTP requests","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-http-node"}},{"id":"ml-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a Create generative answers node, you clear 'Send a message' and store the response in a global variable. Why?","options":["To disable the knowledge source entirely","To prevent the agent from immediately returning the answer so you can customize it (for example, render it in an Adaptive Card)","To force the answer to be returned twice","To switch the agent to classic authoring"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Customize generative answers","sub":null,"explanation":"Clearing 'Send a message' and storing the generated answer in a global variable stops the agent from immediately returning the answer, letting you customize it—such as rendering it on an Adaptive Card via a Power Fx formula.","source":{"title":"Use generative answers in a topic","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-node"}},{"id":"ml-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A maker needs precise, structured JSON output with full control over tone and constraints, beyond what the orchestrator provides. Which Copilot Studio capability fits best?","options":["A generative answers node over a public website","An AI prompt (custom prompt) tool","A Condition node","The Conversational boosting system topic"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Custom prompts as tools","sub":null,"explanation":"AI prompts (custom prompts) give makers full control over the model's input—tone, constraints, examples, and structured output formats such as JSON—making them ideal when the orchestrator's fixed system prompt isn't precise enough.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"ml-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When a generative answers node defines its own knowledge source AND the agent has agent-level knowledge sources, which takes priority?","options":["Agent-level sources always win","Knowledge sources defined in the generative answers node take priority; agent-level sources act as a fallback","They are merged with equal weight","Neither is used unless web search is enabled"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Generative answers priority","sub":null,"explanation":"Knowledge sources defined in a generative answers node take priority over agent-level knowledge sources, which function as a fallback.","source":{"title":"Search public data or use a Bing Custom Search for generative answers nodes","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-generative-answers-bing"}},{"id":"ml-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Your agent needs to ground answers in large bodies of external content (ServiceNow KB, Confluence) with semantic indexing and citations. Which knowledge approach should you use?","options":["Power Platform real-time connectors","Copilot connectors that index data into Microsoft Graph","A Send HTTP request node per query","A computer use tool"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Copilot connectors as knowledge","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot connectors index organizational external data into Microsoft Graph so it can be added as a knowledge source, providing semantic indexing and citations while respecting source-level permissions. They're the right choice for knowledge discovery and grounded Q&A at scale.","source":{"title":"Copilot connectors versus Power Platform connectors as knowledge sources","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-graph-vs-power-platform-connectors"}},{"id":"ml-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When should you use a Power Platform connector (real-time connector) as a knowledge source instead of a Copilot connector?","options":["When you need to fetch live data or act on real-time enterprise systems without copying data into Microsoft 365","When you want to index a large static knowledge base","When you need semantic search across indexed content","When the data must be crawled into Microsoft Graph"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Power Platform connectors as knowledge","sub":null,"explanation":"Power Platform (real-time) connectors fetch live data or perform actions on real-time enterprise systems—such as Salesforce, ServiceNow, or Azure SQL—without replicating data into Microsoft 365. Copilot connectors are for index-then-answer scenarios at scale.","source":{"title":"Copilot connectors versus Power Platform connectors as knowledge sources","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-graph-vs-power-platform-connectors"}},{"id":"ml-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When adding Azure AI Search as a knowledge source in Copilot Studio, what does Microsoft recommend about the connection?","options":["Manually configure an endpoint and API key in a variable","Add it through a formal data connection (for example, using Microsoft Entra ID authentication), not a manually configured endpoint and key","Use a Send HTTP request node to query the index","Add as many vector indexes as you need"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Add Azure AI Search","sub":null,"explanation":"You must add Azure AI Search through a formal data connection (Data sources > Azure AI Search), ideally with Entra ID authentication—not a manually configured endpoint and API key. Only one vector index can be added per connection.","source":{"title":"Add Azure AI Search as a knowledge source","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"ml-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Compared with Dataverse or Graph-connector knowledge, what is a distinguishing characteristic of an Azure AI Search index knowledge source in Copilot Studio?","options":["It requires the user to be authenticated with Entra ID for delegated calls","It returns results from a linked vectorized index, and the connection isn't delegated (no per-user security trimming)","It only supports structured Dataverse tables","It can't be used with generative answers"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Azure AI Search RAG behavior","sub":null,"explanation":"Azure AI Search returns results from a linked vectorized index. Unlike Dataverse tables or Graph connectors (which use Entra ID delegated auth), the Azure AI Search connection isn't delegated—there is no security trimming or per-user authentication requirement.","source":{"title":"Enhance AI responses by using Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/retrieval-augmented-generation"}},{"id":"ml-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When is the computer use tool the most appropriate choice for extending an agent?","options":["When the target system exposes a clean REST API","When you need to automate a website or desktop app that has no API, by visually interpreting the screen and using a virtual mouse and keyboard","When you only need to index documents for knowledge","When you want to deploy to Microsoft Teams"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Computer use tool","sub":null,"explanation":"Computer use lets an agent interact with websites and desktop apps by visually interpreting the screen and using a virtual mouse and keyboard. It's ideal when no API is available—legacy or desktop-only workflows—such as data entry, invoice processing, and extraction.","source":{"title":"Automate web and desktop apps with computer use","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/computer-use"}},{"id":"ml-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a prerequisite for using the computer use tool in a Copilot Studio agent?","options":["The agent must use classic orchestration","The agent must have generative orchestration turned on","The agent must be published to Facebook","A premium Power Automate license per user"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Computer use prerequisite","sub":null,"explanation":"Computer use is only available for agents with generative orchestration turned on. It's powered by a Computer-Using Agent (CUA) model that perceives the screen, reasons, and acts.","source":{"title":"Automate web and desktop apps with computer use","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/computer-use"}},{"id":"ml-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What does connecting an agent to a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server give the agent access to in Copilot Studio?","options":["Only static text knowledge","Tools and resources published by the server, kept dynamically in sync as they change","A dedicated virtual machine","The ability to bypass data loss prevention policies"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"MCP capabilities","sub":null,"explanation":"An MCP server exposes tools (functions the model can call) and resources (file-like data). Copilot Studio dynamically reflects changes to the server's tools and resources, so the agent always has the latest versions and obsolete items are removed.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with Model Context Protocol","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-mcp"}},{"id":"ml-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which setting must be enabled on an agent to use MCP tools?","options":["Generative orchestration","Classic chatbot mode","No authentication","Web search"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"MCP prerequisite","sub":null,"explanation":"You must turn on generative orchestration to use MCP. Copilot Studio currently supports MCP tools and resources (not prompts).","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with Model Context Protocol","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-mcp"}},{"id":"ml-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How are MCP servers governed in Copilot Studio with respect to Power Platform data policies?","options":["MCP servers are exempt from data loss prevention policies","Because MCP connectivity relies on Power Platform connectors, a data policy that regulates connectors also regulates the MCP server and its tools","MCP servers require a separate Microsoft 365 DLP policy","MCP servers can only be governed in the Microsoft 365 admin center"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"MCP and data policies","sub":null,"explanation":"Access to MCP servers relies on Power Platform connectors for connectivity. Therefore, if a data policy regulates Power Platform connectors, it also regulates access to the MCP server and its tools.","source":{"title":"Add tools and resources from an MCP server to your agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/mcp-add-components-to-agent"}},{"id":"ml-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When adding a REST API as a tool in Copilot Studio, which OpenAPI specification version must you provide?","options":["OpenAPI v3 only","OpenAPI v2 (a v3 spec is automatically translated to v2)","WSDL","GraphQL schema"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"REST API tool spec version","sub":null,"explanation":"You must create REST API tools from an OpenAPI v2 specification because of how Power Platform processes API specs; if you submit a v3 spec, the creation process automatically translates it to v2.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"ml-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"On the Tools card for a Microsoft 365 Copilot agent, which of the following is a valid 'Create new' tool type?","options":["Sensitivity label","Computer use","Retention policy","Conditional Access policy"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Tool types","sub":null,"explanation":"When adding a new tool you can create: Prompt, Agent flow, Computer use, Custom connector, Model Context Protocol, and REST API. Sensitivity labels, retention, and Conditional Access are governance/security features, not Copilot Studio tools.","source":{"title":"Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot with agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/microsoft-copilot-extend-copilot-extensions"}},{"id":"ml-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What three things must you provide to connect an agent to a REST API in Copilot Studio?","options":["An OpenAPI spec, authentication details, and descriptions that help the model decide when to invoke the API","A Dataverse table, a sensitivity label, and a retention policy","A virtual network, a private endpoint, and a service principal","A test set, a pipeline, and an environment variable"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"REST API tool inputs","sub":null,"explanation":"To connect to a REST API you provide an OpenAPI specification defining the API's actions, the authentication type and details, and descriptions that help the language model determine when to invoke the API.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"ml-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where in Copilot Studio do you add another existing agent so your main agent can delegate to it?","options":["On the Knowledge page, as a knowledge source","On the Agents page for your main agent, by selecting Add an agent","In the Power Platform admin center pipelines","In the Topics code editor only"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Connect existing Copilot Studio agent","sub":null,"explanation":"Multi-agent collaboration is configured on the Agents page for your main agent: select Add an agent and choose the agent type (child agent, existing Copilot Studio agent, Foundry, Fabric, A2A, etc.).","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns and best practices","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"ml-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why is it important to write a clear, specific description when adding a connected agent to your main agent?","options":["The description is shown to end users as a disclaimer","The main agent's orchestrator uses the description to understand when to invoke the connected agent","It sets the localStorage key","It determines the connector response size limit"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Effective agent metadata","sub":null,"explanation":"The description should explain the purpose of the connected agent so the main agent's orchestrator can understand when that agent should be used—especially important when multiple tools or agents have overlapping descriptions.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Microsoft Foundry agent (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"ml-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When creating a connection to a Microsoft Foundry agent from Copilot Studio, what must you supply?","options":["Only the agent's display name","The Foundry project endpoint URL (to create the connection) and the Agent Id of the Foundry agent","A SharePoint site URL","An Azure AI Search admin key"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Connect Foundry agent","sub":null,"explanation":"To connect a Foundry agent you create a connection using the Foundry project endpoint URL, then enter the Agent Id of the Foundry agent (which must be created in the new Microsoft Foundry portal) plus a name and description.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Microsoft Foundry agent (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"ml-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Before you can connect a Microsoft Fabric data agent to a Copilot Studio agent, which condition must be met?","options":["The Fabric data agent must be published, and both it and the Copilot Studio agent must be on the same tenant","The Fabric data agent must be in draft state","The Copilot Studio agent must use classic orchestration","You must disable generative AI orchestration"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Connect Fabric data agent","sub":null,"explanation":"The Fabric data agent must be published with a detailed description, both resources must be on the same tenant, you must be signed in with the same account that has access, and generative orchestration must be enabled.","source":{"title":"Consume a Fabric Data Agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-science/data-agent-microsoft-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"ml-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When you add a Fabric data agent to a Copilot Studio agent, which authentication options can you choose for the connected agent?","options":["Anonymous only","User authentication or Agent author authentication","API key only","Certificate only"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Fabric data agent authentication","sub":null,"explanation":"For a connected Fabric data agent you can choose User authentication or Agent author authentication. With User authentication you must ensure users have access to the Fabric data agent and its underlying data sources.","source":{"title":"Consume a Fabric Data Agent in Microsoft Copilot Studio (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-science/data-agent-microsoft-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"ml-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why would you connect to an external agent over the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol instead of a plain HTTP connector?","options":["A2A is faster for one-shot API calls","A2A is designed for agent workflows, supports multiturn interactions, and provides rich contextual metadata for delegating tasks","A2A requires no endpoint URL","HTTP connectors can't return JSON"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"A2A vs HTTP","sub":null,"explanation":"Unlike an HTTP connector, the A2A protocol is designed for agent workflows: it supports multiturn interactions, provides rich contextual metadata, and is interoperable across frameworks—so Copilot Studio can delegate tasks to another agent, not just call APIs.","source":{"title":"Connect an agent available over the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-agent-to-agent"}},{"id":"ml-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How does a maker use a frontier model from the Azure AI Foundry model catalog in a Copilot Studio prompt?","options":["By pasting the model weights into the prompt","In the prompt's Model field, select '+' to connect a model from Azure AI Foundry, entering the model deployment name and base model name exactly as shown in Foundry","By enabling web search","Foundry models can't be used in prompts"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Bring your own model","sub":null,"explanation":"In a prompt tool, select the Model dropdown and the plus sign to connect a model from Azure AI Foundry; enter the Model deployment name and Base model name exactly as they appear in Azure AI Foundry, then Connect. The prompt then always uses that model.","source":{"title":"Bring your own model for your prompts","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/bring-your-own-model-prompts"}},{"id":"ml-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where can an administrator apply a data loss prevention (DLP) policy specifically for the Azure AI Foundry models used in prompts?","options":["In the Microsoft 365 admin center under Copilot","In the Power Platform admin center data policies, under the 'Azure AI Foundry' connector","In the SharePoint admin center","In Microsoft Purview only"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Govern Foundry model connector","sub":null,"explanation":"Azure AI Foundry models are connected via connectors, so admins set governance for the 'Azure AI Foundry' connector on the data policies page in the Power Platform admin center, adding a specific DLP policy for these models.","source":{"title":"Bring your own model for your prompts","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/bring-your-own-model-prompts"}},{"id":"ml-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A team wants generative answers grounded on a developer-built search index within their own geo, while still letting the AI summarize a moderated answer. Which option fits?","options":["Use Bing Search grounding","Use an Azure AI Search index as a knowledge source","Use a Power Automate desktop flow","Disable generative answers"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Azure AI Search for grounding","sub":null,"explanation":"Using an Azure AI Search index as a knowledge source lets developers build their own indexes (searching content within their geos) while still using the generative answers feature to craft a moderated, summarized answer.","source":{"title":"Use public websites to improve generative answers","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/generative-ai-public-websites"}},{"id":"ml-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How do you connect a Copilot Studio agent to Azure Application Insights to capture telemetry?","options":["Add the Application Insights connection string in the agent's Settings > Advanced, in the Application Insights section","Add a Send HTTP request node pointing to Azure Monitor","Install a Power Platform pipeline","Enable web search"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Connect Application Insights","sub":null,"explanation":"You connect an agent to Application Insights by entering the connection string under the agent's Settings > Advanced > Application Insights section. You can optionally enable logging of activities and sensitive activity properties.","source":{"title":"Capture telemetry with Application Insights","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/advanced-bot-framework-composer-capture-telemetry"}},{"id":"ml-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After deploying a solution between environments, which Copilot Studio setting is NOT solution-aware and must be reconfigured post-deployment?","options":["Topics","Agent flows","Azure Application Insights settings","Variables"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"ALM non-solution-aware items","sub":null,"explanation":"Some Copilot Studio items aren't solution-aware and need post-deployment steps on the downstream environment, including Azure Application Insights settings, manual authentication settings, Direct Line/Web channel security, deployed channels, and sharing.","source":{"title":"Establish an application lifecycle management strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"ml-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In Copilot Studio agent evaluation, what is a test set?","options":["A single question with no expected answer","A group of test cases, where each test case simulates a user interaction and can include an expected response","A list of channels the agent is published to","A pipeline stage"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Test case and test set","sub":null,"explanation":"A test case is a single interaction simulating how a user interacts with the agent and can include the expected response. A group of test cases is a test set, letting you run many cases at once and get an aggregate score.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"ml-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which are valid ways to build a test set in Copilot Studio agent evaluation?","options":["Only by writing each case manually","Generate cases from knowledge or topics, import a test set file, or manually write cases","Only by importing from Azure DevOps","Only by recording a live conversation"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Create a test set","sub":null,"explanation":"You can generate test cases from knowledge or topics, import a test set file, or manually write test cases. Each test set can evaluate the agent using multiple test methods at once.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"ml-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After running an evaluation with a test set, how does Copilot Studio score the results?","options":["It assigns a single letter grade","Each test case gets Pass, Fail, Invalid, or Error, and the test set gets a Pass rate based on the pass/fail rate","It only reports total latency","It compares against the 700 passing score"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Evaluation scoring","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio simulates conversations, collects responses, and assigns each test case a Pass, Fail, Invalid, or Error based on the case criteria; the test set receives a Pass rate based on the pass/fail rate, visible under Recent results.","source":{"title":"Run evaluations and view results","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-results"}},{"id":"ml-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You want to measure whether an agent change improved quality and keep the evidence longer than the default. What should you do?","options":["Run the same test set at least twice and use 'Compare with'; export results to CSV since results are kept only 89 days","Delete the old test set and create a new one","Increase the connector response limit","Switch the agent to classic orchestration"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Compare and retain results","sub":null,"explanation":"Run the same test set at least twice to use the 'Compare with' tool, which shows which cases improved or declined. Test results are available in Copilot Studio for 89 days; export to CSV to keep them longer.","source":{"title":"Run evaluations and view results","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-results"}},{"id":"ml-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where is an agent stored when you create it in Copilot Studio, and why does that matter for ALM?","options":["In SharePoint; it enables versioning","In a Power Platform solution; solutions are the mechanism to transport agents and components across environments","In a Teams channel; it enables sharing","In Application Insights; it enables monitoring"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Agents and solutions","sub":null,"explanation":"When you create an agent in Copilot Studio it's created within a Power Platform solution. Solutions are the mechanism for ALM—containers used to export/import and transport artifacts and customizations across environments.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"ml-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which of the following is one of the ALM golden rules for Copilot Studio agents?","options":["Always customize directly in production","Always work in the context of solutions and use a custom publisher and prefix","Never use environment variables","Export solutions as unmanaged into production"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"ALM golden rules","sub":null,"explanation":"ALM golden rules include: don't customize outside a development environment, always work in the context of solutions, use a custom publisher and prefix, use environment variables for cross-environment settings, and export/deploy as managed (except dev).","source":{"title":"Establish an application lifecycle management strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"ml-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why use environment variables in a Copilot Studio solution?","options":["To store the agent's published transcript","To store parameter keys/values separately from consuming objects so values can change per environment when migrating solutions","To bypass DLP policies","To increase the connector response limit"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Environment variables","sub":null,"explanation":"Environment variables store parameter keys and values that serve as input to other application objects. Separating parameters from the consuming objects lets you change values within or across environments when you migrate solutions.","source":{"title":"Recommendations for designing a deployment failure mitigation strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/well-architected/operational-excellence/mitigation-strategy"}},{"id":"ml-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Among Copilot Studio ALM automation options, which is best suited for empowering citizen developers with low setup complexity?","options":["Azure DevOps","GitHub Actions for Power Platform","Power Platform Pipelines for Copilot Studio","A manual export/import each time"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Power Platform Pipelines","sub":null,"explanation":"Power Platform Pipelines bring CI/CD into the service with low setup complexity, ideal for empowering citizen developers. Azure DevOps targets enterprise teams needing full control; GitHub Actions targets dev/admin teams.","source":{"title":"Establish an application lifecycle management strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"ml-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where can a maker configure and manage solution pipelines without leaving Copilot Studio?","options":["In the solution explorer, by selecting Pipelines underneath the list of solutions","Only in the Microsoft 365 admin center","Only in Azure DevOps","In the Topics code editor"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Configure pipelines","sub":null,"explanation":"Solution pipelines automate deployment of solutions across environments. In Copilot Studio you open the solution explorer and select Pipelines underneath the list of solutions, then follow the Power Apps pipeline setup requirements.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"ml-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What can a single agent evaluation test case represent in Copilot Studio?","options":["Only a single question with no expected answer","A single question or an entire conversation, optionally including the expected response","Only a published channel configuration","Only a pipeline deployment"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Multi-turn test cases","sub":null,"explanation":"A test case is a single interaction that simulates how a user would interact with the agent. The interaction can be a single question or an entire conversation, and it can also include the answer you expect the agent to reply with.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"ml-54","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When reviewing evaluation results, a failing test case may stem from two perspectives. Which pair correctly describes them?","options":["A licensing issue or a channel issue","A test case issue (prompt/expected answer/criteria) or an agent design issue (instructions, knowledge, or tool configuration)","A pipeline issue or an environment variable issue","A connector limit issue or an authentication issue"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Root cause analysis","sub":null,"explanation":"Assess failed cases from two perspectives: a test case issue (is the prompt, expected answer, or acceptance criteria causing the failure?) or an agent design issue (unclear instructions, or flaws in knowledge or tool configuration?). Then refine the test case or improve the agent.","source":{"title":"Review the agent evaluation checklist","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agents/agent-evaluation/evaluation-checklist"}},{"id":"ml-55","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Following the ALM golden rules, how should you deploy a Copilot Studio solution to test and production environments?","options":["As unmanaged, so makers can edit directly there","As managed, reserving unmanaged solutions for development environments","It doesn't matter which solution type you use","Always as a single default solution"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Managed vs unmanaged","sub":null,"explanation":"An ALM golden rule is to export and deploy solutions as managed, unless you're setting up a development environment. You shouldn't customize outside of development; downstream environments receive managed solutions.","source":{"title":"Establish an application lifecycle management strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"ml-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which integration option in Copilot Studio is described as a no-code/low-code wrapper for a REST API that can be created once and used across many agents?","options":["A Send HTTP request node","A custom connector","A Bot Framework skill","A generative answers node"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Reusable integration","sub":null,"explanation":"Custom connectors are no-code/low-code wrappers for REST APIs that can be created once and reused across many agents, with built-in parsing and error handling.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"ml-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which user variables become available in the authoring canvas when you choose 'Authenticate with Microsoft'?","options":["User.AccessToken and User.IsLoggedIn","User.ID and User.DisplayName","User.Email and User.Manager","No user variables are available"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Authentication scope","sub":null,"explanation":"With 'Authenticate with Microsoft', User.ID and User.DisplayName are available. User.AccessToken and User.IsLoggedIn are only available with the 'Authenticate manually' option.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"ml-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement about Copilot Studio generative features and customer data is correct?","options":["Agents contribute tenant data to model training by default","No customer data trains the language models, and responses go through grounding validation and harmful-content moderation","Moderation only applies to public-website knowledge","Customer Lockbox is unavailable for Copilot Studio"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Responsible AI governance","sub":null,"explanation":"Agents don't contribute tenant data to model training. Each response goes through automated validation: moderation of harmful/noncompliant/copyrighted content and grounding validation to remove incorrect information.","source":{"title":"Enhance AI responses by using Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/retrieval-augmented-generation"}},{"id":"ml-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"An agent flow or workflow consists of at minimum which two elements?","options":["Two connectors","A trigger and at least one action","A topic and a variable","A prompt and a knowledge source"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Trigger and action","sub":null,"explanation":"An agent flow or workflow consists of a trigger (the event that starts it) and at least one action (a task it performs in response).","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"ml-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which capability is true of agent flows that helps with monitoring and governance?","options":["Their activity can be monitored separately, and they can hide secrets by retrieving credentials from Azure Key Vault","They cannot be placed inside solutions","They run only on a per-user license model","They expose all keys in plain text"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Monitoring agent flows","sub":null,"explanation":"Agent flows can have their activity monitored separately and can hide sensitive values such as keys and secrets, getting credentials from Azure Key Vault for authentication.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"ml-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Copilot Studio supports Adaptive Cards schema up to which version, and what is a host limitation to remember?","options":["Up to 1.6, but Teams and the live chat widget are limited to 1.5","Up to 2.0 everywhere","Only 1.0 in all channels","There is no version limit"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"Adaptive Card schema","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio supports Adaptive Cards schema 1.6 and earlier, but the targeted host matters: Teams and the live chat widget are limited to 1.5, and the default Web Chat supports 1.6 but not Action.Execute.","source":{"title":"Using Adaptive Cards in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/adaptive-cards-overview"}},{"id":"ml-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a Send HTTP request node, how can you build a JSON body that includes dynamic values from variables?","options":["JSON bodies must be static literals","Select Edit JSON, choose Formula, and enter a Power Fx object that is converted to JSON when the request is made","Use a desktop flow","Reference an Azure AI Search index"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1C","topic":"HTTP request body","sub":null,"explanation":"For JSON content you can switch to a Power Fx formula (Edit JSON > Formula). Any existing JSON is converted to a Power Fx object, letting you include dynamic values and reference variables; it's converted back to JSON when the request runs.","source":{"title":"Make HTTP requests","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-http-node"}},{"id":"ml-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What do Copilot connectors do with external enterprise data so it can be used as agent knowledge?","options":["They copy data into Dataverse tables","They ingest and index data into Microsoft Graph while respecting source-level permissions","They expose data only through HTTP request nodes","They store data in Application Insights"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Copilot connectors and Graph","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot connectors ingest and index data from external sources into Microsoft Graph, adding knowledge to Copilot. They fully respect source-level permissions so users only access content they're authorized to view.","source":{"title":"Add Copilot connectors as a knowledge source","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-connectors"}},{"id":"ml-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When adding an Azure AI Search knowledge source, how many vector indexes can be added per connection?","options":["Unlimited","Exactly one vector index","Up to 15","Up to 100"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Azure AI Search index limit","sub":null,"explanation":"Only one vector index can be added when configuring an Azure AI Search knowledge source. After adding, the status shows In progress while indexing, then Ready.","source":{"title":"Add Azure AI Search as a knowledge source","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"ml-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"The computer use tool follows an iterative loop. Which three steps make up that loop?","options":["Index, query, summarize","Perception, reasoning, action","Trigger, action, respond","Plan, build, publish"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Computer use loop","sub":null,"explanation":"Computer use follows a loop of Perception (capture screenshots), Reasoning (evaluate state and prior actions), and Action (click, type, scroll) until the task is complete or needs user input.","source":{"title":"FAQ for the computer use tool","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/faqs-computer-use"}},{"id":"ml-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is the simplest, recommended way to connect an agent to an existing MCP server in Copilot Studio?","options":["Manually edit YAML in the code editor","Use the MCP onboarding wizard from Tools > Add a tool > Model Context Protocol","Create an Azure DevOps pipeline","Add it as an Azure AI Search index"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"MCP onboarding","sub":null,"explanation":"The recommended method is the MCP onboarding wizard: on the agent's Tools page, select Add a tool > New tool > Model Context Protocol, then fill in server name, description, URL, and authentication.","source":{"title":"Connect your agent to an existing Model Context Protocol (MCP) server","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/mcp-add-existing-server-to-agent"}},{"id":"ml-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why does the tool 'Description for the agent to know when to use this tool' matter when adding a tool to an agent?","options":["It is shown to end users as legal text","It is a conversational description that helps Microsoft 365 Copilot decide when to invoke the tool and which questions trigger it","It sets the passing score","It determines the channel"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Tool details configuration","sub":null,"explanation":"The tool description is a full conversational description of when Copilot should use the tool and the questions that trigger it; together with the tool name it helps Copilot choose the right tool to execute.","source":{"title":"Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot with agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/microsoft-copilot-extend-copilot-extensions"}},{"id":"ml-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which of the following is a supported way to extend an agent with another agent in Copilot Studio?","options":["Add a child agent, connect to an existing Copilot Studio agent, or connect to a Foundry/Fabric/A2A agent","Only A2A connections are supported","Only child agents are supported","Agents can't call other agents"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Add other agents","sub":null,"explanation":"Multi-agent collaboration supports adding a child agent, connecting to an existing Copilot Studio agent, and connecting to external agents such as Microsoft Foundry, Microsoft Fabric data agents, Microsoft 365 Agents SDK agents, and A2A agents.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns and best practices","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"ml-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When connecting to a Microsoft Foundry agent from Copilot Studio, which Foundry agents can you connect to?","options":["Only agents created in the previous (classic) portal","Only agents created in the new Microsoft Foundry portal","Any agent regardless of portal version","Only agents exposed over A2A"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Foundry portal requirement","sub":null,"explanation":"You can only connect to Foundry agents created in the new Microsoft Foundry portal. Connecting to an agent created in the previous portal results in a '404 - Version not found' error.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Microsoft Foundry agent (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"ml-115","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which kind of information does a typical A2A message payload include that an HTTP connector does not provide?","options":["Only the latest user utterance","A unique contextId, message IDs, locale, full chat history, and message content parts","Just an API key","Only billing data"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"A2A metadata payload","sub":null,"explanation":"An A2A message payload includes structured metadata such as a unique contextId, message IDs, locale information, the full chat history (not just the latest utterance), and message content parts—context the agent can use for downstream logic.","source":{"title":"Connect an agent available over the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-agent-to-agent"}},{"id":"ml-116","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What underpins prompts (custom prompts) in Copilot Studio, enabling use of frontier models?","options":["Models powered by Azure Foundry, with native integration to the Azure AI Foundry model catalog","On-device models only","A local Power Automate engine","Bing Search grounding only"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Foundry models in prompts","sub":null,"explanation":"Prompts use models powered by Azure Foundry. The native Azure AI Foundry integration brings frontier models (GPT, Llama, DeepSeek, and 1,800+ catalog models) into Copilot Studio prompts.","source":{"title":"Overview of prompts","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/prompts-overview"}},{"id":"ml-117","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What does the Application Insights 'Agent details' view provide for AI agents?","options":["Only cost data for Copilot Studio","A unified monitoring experience across Microsoft Foundry, Copilot Studio, and third-party agents, consolidating telemetry to track performance, token usage/cost, and errors","A way to publish agents to Teams","A replacement for solutions"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2D","topic":"Unified agent monitoring","sub":null,"explanation":"The Agent details view in Application Insights gives a unified experience for monitoring AI agents across Microsoft Foundry, Copilot Studio, and third-party agents, consolidating telemetry to track performance, analyze token usage and cost, and troubleshoot errors.","source":{"title":"Monitor AI agents with Application Insights","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-monitor/app/agents-view"}},{"id":"ml-118","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Because agents are probabilistic, what does Microsoft recommend to get reliable evaluation results?","options":["Run each test set once and trust the result","Run each test set multiple times and calculate the average success rate, aiming for a realistic pass rate of about 80–90%","Require 100% pass before deploying","Use only a single test case"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Evaluation pass rate target","sub":null,"explanation":"Because agents can produce varying responses to the same prompt, you should run each test set multiple times and calculate the average success rate, aiming for a realistic pass rate of 80–90% based on business needs.","source":{"title":"Review the agent evaluation checklist","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agents/agent-evaluation/evaluation-checklist"}},{"id":"ml-119","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How can agent evaluations be integrated into CI/CD or regression testing workflows?","options":["They cannot be automated","By triggering evaluations programmatically using the Power Platform REST API against predefined test sets","Only by manually re-running them in the UI","By exporting the agent to GitHub"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Automate evaluations","sub":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio lets you run automated tests against predefined test sets using the Power Platform REST API, so you can trigger evaluations programmatically as part of development workflows, release validation, regression testing, and CI/CD pipelines.","source":{"title":"Automate agent evaluations with Power Platform API","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-rest-api"}},{"id":"ml-120","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A healthy ALM strategy for Copilot Studio agents includes at least which environments?","options":["A single production environment","Development, test, and production","Only development and production","One environment per maker"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Healthy ALM environments","sub":null,"explanation":"A healthy ALM strategy includes at least three environments—development, test, and production. Developers promote changes from dev to test, fix any bugs in dev, then deploy to production. Production is a production-type environment; others are sandbox.","source":{"title":"Establish an application lifecycle management strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"ml-121","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In Power Platform ALM, what is the role of solutions?","options":["They store telemetry for Application Insights","They are the mechanism to distribute components (agents, flows, tables, etc.) across environments via export and import","They replace environment variables","They are required only for desktop flows"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Solutions transport components","sub":null,"explanation":"Solutions are the mechanism for implementing ALM: you use them to distribute components—such as tables, apps, flows, and agents—across environments through export and import. Dataverse stores the solutions and pipelines.","source":{"title":"Overview of application lifecycle management with Microsoft Power Platform","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-platform/alm/overview-alm"}},{"id":"ml-122","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A connector tool returns an HTTP 400 Bad Request in Copilot Studio. What is the cause and the fix?","options":["The connector is premium; switch to standard","The response exceeds the 500 KB connector limit; configure the action inputs to filter the data returned","Generative orchestration is off; turn it on","The flow is synchronous; make it asynchronous"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Connector response limit","sub":null,"explanation":"Connector responses are limited to 500 KB; exceeding it causes an HTTP 400. Configure the connector action inputs to filter the data the service returns so the response stays under the limit.","source":{"title":"Connector request failure","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/power-platform/copilot-studio/actions/connector-request-failure"}},{"id":"ml-123","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When configuring an A2A connection, what happens if the external agent exposes a valid agent card at the standard .well-known location?","options":["Copilot Studio automatically populates the agent's name and description from the card","The connection is blocked for security","You must enter a vector index","The agent is deployed to Teams automatically"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"A2A agent card","sub":null,"explanation":"After you enter the A2A endpoint URL, if the agent has a valid agent card at the standard .well-known URL, Copilot Studio automatically pulls the name and description into the form. A2A connections use the custom connector infrastructure, so they can also reach on-premises or virtual-network agents.","source":{"title":"Connect an agent available over the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-agent-to-agent"}},{"id":"gen-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are planning an agent that must call a leave-balance REST API on behalf of the employee who is chatting, passing that person's own token. Which authentication option must you configure so the agent can obtain a token for the signed-in user?","options":["No authentication","Authenticate with Microsoft","Authenticate manually","Generic OAuth2 with anonymous fallback"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Only Authenticate manually exposes the User.AccessToken variable, which is required to call an API as the signed-in user; Authenticate with Microsoft does not provide User.AccessToken.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"gen-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker switched an agent's authentication option in Settings, but end users continue to experience the previous sign-in behavior. What step was most likely missed?","options":["Adding the user to a security group","Enabling the connector in the DLP policy","Restarting the browser session","Publishing the agent"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Authentication changes take effect only after the agent is published; until then the previously published behavior remains in force.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"gen-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An organization wants an internal agent deployed primarily to Microsoft Teams, with the least setup effort and automatic Microsoft Entra ID sign-in so employees are not re-prompted. Which authentication option best fits?","options":["Authenticate with Microsoft","Authenticate manually with Entra ID V2","Authenticate manually with Generic OAuth2","No authentication"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Authenticate with Microsoft uses Entra ID automatically with zero configuration and avoids re-prompting in Teams, but it is limited to the Teams + M365 Copilot channels.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"gen-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need to restrict an agent so that only specific users in your organization can chat with it. Which configuration achieves this?","options":["No authentication published only to the demo website","Authenticate with Microsoft using agent sharing, or Authenticate manually + Entra ID with Require users to sign in","Generic OAuth2 with a Google provider","Publishing the agent to a custom website only"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Access control over which org users can chat works with Authenticate with Microsoft (via sharing) and Authenticate manually + Entra ID (via Require users to sign in); No auth and Generic OAuth2 cannot restrict which org users chat.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"gen-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team plans to deploy a customer-facing agent to a company marketing site available to the public. Which channel is appropriate for this production external audience?","options":["Demo website","Teams + M365 Copilot","Custom website","SharePoint"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"External customer-facing production deployments use the Custom website channel; the demo website is test-only and Teams/SharePoint target internal audiences.","source":{"title":"Publish and deploy your agent (channels) — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"gen-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During planning, a maker wants to share a link so external stakeholders can try the agent before go-live, and asks whether the demo website can serve as the production endpoint. What should you advise?","options":["Yes, the demo website supports production traffic once published","Yes, but only if authentication is set to No authentication","Yes, after enabling it in the DLP policy","No, the demo website is test-only and a custom website channel should be used for production"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The demo website is intended only for testing, not production; a custom website channel is the production-safe option to share with customers.","source":{"title":"Publish and deploy your agent (channels) — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"gen-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While planning governance, you need to explain what a Power Platform data policy (DLP) can govern for an agent in an environment. Which of the following is a valid example?","options":["Knowledge sources, actions/connectors, HTTP requests, and channel publication","The agent's display name and greeting message","The number of topics an agent may contain","The wording of generative answers returned to users"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Data policies govern per environment items such as authentication, knowledge sources, actions/connectors/skills, HTTP requests, channel publication, Application Insights, and triggers.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"gen-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To ensure an agent promotes cleanly from dev to test to prod, a maker wants to package its components for reuse from the start. Which set of items should be included in the solution?","options":["Published channel links and demo website URLs","Topics, flows, connection references, environment variables, and custom connectors","User accounts and security group memberships","Application Insights dashboards and Sentinel alerts"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Reusable components — topics, flows, connection references, environment variables, and custom connectors — are packaged in a solution so the agent promotes cleanly across environments.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker chose Authenticate with Microsoft and now wants to also publish the same agent to a public custom website. What limitation should they be aware of?","options":["It requires enabling Generic OAuth2 first","It only works after adding a knowledge source","Authenticate with Microsoft supports only Teams + M365 Copilot channels","Custom website publishing requires No authentication"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Authenticate with Microsoft is limited to the Teams + M365 Copilot channels, so it cannot be used to publish the agent to a custom website.","source":{"title":"Configure user authentication — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/configuration-end-user-authentication"}},{"id":"gen-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While planning, you must decide how the agent will retrieve a signed-in user's order history from an external system by taking an action at runtime, rather than grounding on documents. Which planning surface does this correspond to?","options":["A tool/action such as a connector, REST/HTTP call, or MCP tool","A knowledge source such as SharePoint or Azure AI Search","A channel configuration","A data loss prevention policy"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Taking an action against a system at runtime is planned as a tool/action (connectors, custom connectors, REST/HTTP, MCP tools, computer use); knowledge sources are used to ground answers, not take actions.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"gen-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent needs to answer employee questions grounded in the content of an existing HR SharePoint site. Which planning surface should you select for this requirement?","options":["A custom connector action","A Generic OAuth2 provider","An Azure Bot Service channel","A knowledge source for grounding/RAG"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Grounding answers in existing content such as a SharePoint site is planned as a knowledge source used for grounding/RAG, distinct from tools that take actions.","source":{"title":"Plan and design integration strategies — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/integrations"}},{"id":"gen-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A tenant administrator wants to prevent makers across the organization from publishing generative-AI agents until governance controls are in place. Which capability supports this?","options":["Sharing the agent with a limited security group","An admin control to disable publishing of generative-AI agents tenant-wide","Setting the agent to No authentication","Removing the demo website channel"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Administrators can disable publishing of generative-AI agents tenant-wide as an environment/governance control, giving makers a safe build space without shipping.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities and responsible AI — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"gen-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Your governance plan requires that maker activity on agents be auditable for compliance review. Which service provides the maker audit logs?","options":["Microsoft Sentinel","Application Insights","Microsoft Purview","Azure Bot Service"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Maker audit logs are provided through Microsoft Purview; Sentinel is used for alerts and Application Insights for telemetry.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities and responsible AI — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"gen-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An enterprise wants a central control plane that treats agents as Microsoft Entra identities so it can apply Conditional Access policies to them. Which capability should the plan reference?","options":["Agent 365","Customer Lockbox","Generic OAuth2","Demo website channel"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Agent 365 acts as a central control plane where agents are given Entra identities so Conditional Access and central governance can be applied.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities and responsible AI — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"gen-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are planning a question node that offers suggested actions and must render consistently in Microsoft Teams. What channel-specific limit should you account for?","options":["Adaptive cards cannot render in Teams at all","Teams requires No authentication for suggested actions","Suggested actions are unavailable outside the demo website","Teams caps a question node at 6 suggested actions"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Channel UX differs by surface; Teams caps a question node at 6 suggested actions, which must be considered when planning the conversation design.","source":{"title":"Publish and deploy your agent (channels) — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"gen-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is preparing to make an agent available across several channels for the first time. What is the correct sequencing for deployment?","options":["Add all channels first, then publish each channel individually","Publish the agent once first, then add channels; publishing pushes the latest content to all connected channels","Configure DLP policies only after the agent is live in production","Enable the demo website last so it becomes the production endpoint"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You publish the agent once first and then add channels; a publish pushes the latest content to all connected channels.","source":{"title":"Publish and deploy your agent (channels) — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/publication-fundamentals-publish-channels"}},{"id":"gen-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is deciding whether to build a piece of automation as an agent flow or a topic. The requirement is that the same set of inputs must always produce exactly the same output, following fixed rules with no variation. Which component fits this requirement?","options":["A topic, because topics guarantee deterministic outcomes","An agent flow, because flows are deterministic rule-based automation","Generative orchestration, because it repeats the same tool each time","A trigger phrase, because it always maps to one response"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An agent flow is deterministic automation — same input produces the same output. A topic handles the conversation, not deterministic rule-based work.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are building an agent flow and want the agent to be able to invoke it as a tool through generative orchestration. Which trigger must you configure on the flow?","options":["When an agent calls the flow","A scheduled (recurrence) trigger","A manual/instant trigger","An event-based trigger"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The \"When an agent calls the flow\" trigger is what makes a flow addable as a tool that the agent can invoke.","source":{"title":"Modify an existing flow to use with an agent — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flow-modify-use-with-agent"}},{"id":"gen-19","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent flow must pause and wait for a manager to approve a leave request before it continues. Which category of action provides this behavior?","options":["A built-in loop action that repeats until approved","A connector action to Microsoft Teams","A Human-in-the-loop action, such as an approval","An AI capability action that generates a reply"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Human-in-the-loop actions (approvals and request-info) pause the flow so a person can respond before it proceeds.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs an agent flow to return a result — the final request status — back to the agent that called it. How is this value passed out of the flow?","options":["By storing it in a system topic variable","Through the flow's output parameters","By posting it with a connector action","Through the flow's trigger phrases"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Output parameters return results from a flow; those outputs become new variables available to the agent.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A topic needs to call an agent flow and supply the employee name and number of days the flow requires. How does the topic get those values into the flow?","options":["By adding the values as trigger phrases on the topic","By writing them to the flow's run history","By mapping agent variables to the flow's input parameters","By storing them in the flow's output parameters"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Input parameters pass values into a flow, and a matching agent variable feeds each flow input parameter.","source":{"title":"Modify an existing flow to use with an agent — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flow-modify-use-with-agent"}},{"id":"gen-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An administrator wants to review why several agent flow runs failed and check how much capacity the flow is consuming. Where should they look?","options":["In the flow's input parameter definitions","In the topic authoring canvas trigger phrases","In the agent's knowledge sources list","In the flow run history and the Power Platform admin center"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Run history shows run status and failures, and capacity usage is monitored in the Power Platform admin center (Licensing → Copilot Studio).","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker converted an existing Power Automate cloud flow into an agent flow. They now want to convert it back to a standard Power Automate flow. What is the outcome?","options":["Conversion is one-way; it cannot be converted back","It can be converted back freely at any time","It can be converted back only within the same solution","It can be converted back only if it has no connector actions"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Converting a Power Automate flow to an agent flow is a one-way operation because the billing model changes.","source":{"title":"Modify an existing flow to use with an agent — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flow-modify-use-with-agent"}},{"id":"gen-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During development, a maker runs an agent flow several times to test its approval logic. What is the effect on Copilot Studio capacity?","options":["Each test run consumes one autonomous action credit","Test runs are blocked until the flow is published","Test runs consume double capacity while in draft","Test runs are free and do not consume capacity"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Every production action consumes capacity, but test runs are free.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent flow must repeat the same action once for every item in a list of order lines. Which type of action provides this capability?","options":["A Human-in-the-loop request-info action","A connector action to a data source","An AI capability that generates a reply","A built-in loop action"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Loops are built-in actions (along with branches, data operations, date/time, and child flows) used to repeat an action across items.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants an agent flow to post a message to a Microsoft Teams channel after an approval is granted. Which action type is used to interact with Teams?","options":["A connector action","A Human-in-the-loop action","A built-in data operation action","A trigger action"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Connector actions (Microsoft 365, third-party, and custom) let a flow interact with external services such as Teams.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to version their agent flows, export them between environments, and manage them as part of an ALM strategy. Where should the flows live?","options":["In the agent's trigger phrase list","In a Dataverse table row per flow","In a solution","In the flow's run history"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Flows live in solutions, which provide drafts, versioning, and export/import for application lifecycle management.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is describing the difference between an agent flow and a topic to a colleague. Which statement is accurate?","options":["A topic acts deterministically while a flow talks to the user","Both a topic and a flow are chosen by classic trigger phrases","A topic talks (handles the conversation) while a flow acts (does the work)","A flow handles the conversation while a topic performs connector calls"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Where a topic talks (drives the conversation), a flow acts — running deterministic automation such as connectors, approvals, and loops.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent flow calls an external connector that occasionally fails. The maker wants the flow to detect the failure and take an alternate path instead of stopping abruptly. Which approach should they use in the flow designer?","options":["Add trigger phrases that describe the failure","Use conditions/branches and run-after settings to handle the error","Convert the flow back to a Power Automate flow","Remove the output parameters so no result is returned"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Error handling in agent flows uses conditions/branches and run-after settings so failures are caught gracefully.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In generative orchestration, an agent decides which topics, tools, and knowledge to use. What determines whether it chooses to call a particular agent flow that has been added as a tool?","options":["The flow's description, which the agent reads to pick it","The number of input parameters the flow defines","The order in which the flow appears in the solution","The flow's most recent run history entry"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Generative orchestration selects topics, tools, and knowledge from their descriptions, so a clear flow/tool description is what drives selection.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants an agent flow to run automatically every morning at 7 AM to summarize the previous day's tickets, with no user or agent involvement to start it. Which trigger type is appropriate?","options":["The When an agent calls the flow trigger","A manual/instant trigger","A Human-in-the-loop trigger","A scheduled trigger"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A scheduled trigger runs a flow automatically on a recurrence, such as every morning, without manual or agent invocation.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent invokes a flow through generative orchestration, and the flow runs several actions. How is this billed against Copilot Studio capacity?","options":["As a Classic answer plus the individual actions","As an Autonomous action plus the individual actions","As a single flat charge regardless of the number of actions","It is free because generative orchestration invoked it"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When a flow is invoked via generative orchestration, it is billed as an Autonomous action plus each action it runs; invocation from a topic is billed as a Classic answer plus actions.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are configuring an agent that uses generative orchestration. How does the agent decide which topic to run for a given user message?","options":["It matches the user's request against each topic's description and picks the best fit.","It compares the user's input to each topic's trigger phrases.","It always runs the topic that has the most trigger phrases defined.","It requires the user's message to contain an exact match to the topic name."],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Under generative orchestration the agent chooses the best topic, tool, or knowledge source from each item's description, so writing clear descriptions is what makes a topic selectable.","source":{"title":"Use generative AI to build and manage topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/advanced-generative-actions"}},{"id":"gen-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is building an agent that uses classic orchestration. To make each custom topic match reliably, how many trigger phrases should they add?","options":["Exactly one, matching the topic name.","Two or three short keywords.","About 5 to 10 varied phrases.","At least 50 phrases to cover every wording."],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In classic orchestration NLU matches user input to the closest topic using its trigger phrases; the guidance is to provide roughly 5 to 10 varied phrases (no exact match is needed).","source":{"title":"Create and edit topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-create-edit-topics"}},{"id":"gen-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to remove the built-in Greeting system topic because it clutters the topic list. What is the correct guidance?","options":["Delete it permanently from the topics page like any custom topic.","System topics can't be created or deleted, but you can disable or edit them.","Rename the system topic so it stops being included in orchestration.","Convert it to a custom topic first, then delete the custom copy."],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"System topics (Greeting, Escalate, End of Conversation, Start over, and so on) can't be created or deleted; a maker can only disable or edit them.","source":{"title":"Use system topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-system-topics"}},{"id":"gen-36","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When naming custom topics in Copilot Studio, which character should a maker avoid so that solution export does not break?","options":["Spaces between words.","Uppercase letters.","Numbers at the end of the name.","Periods (.) anywhere in the topic name."],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A period in a topic name breaks solution export, so topic names should avoid the period character.","source":{"title":"Create and edit topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-create-edit-topics"}},{"id":"gen-37","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A topic must ask the user for an order number and store their answer in a variable using a prebuilt entity. Which node performs this slot filling?","options":["A Message node.","A Question node.","A Condition node.","A Set variable node."],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Question node asks the user and captures the input into a variable via an entity (prebuilt or custom) — that is slot filling.","source":{"title":"Use entities and slot filling — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/advanced-entities-slot-filling"}},{"id":"gen-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent must answer open-ended questions from a SharePoint knowledge source when no topic specifically handles the request. Which node should the maker add as a fallback?","options":["A Generative answers node.","An HTTP request node.","A Tool node.","An Adaptive Card node."],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Generative answers node synthesizes a grounded reply from knowledge sources at runtime (RAG), which makes it ideal as a fallback for open questions.","source":{"title":"Use generative answers in a topic (boost node) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-node"}},{"id":"gen-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Inside a topic, a maker needs to call an external REST API and then use the JSON it returns. Which node makes the call?","options":["A Generative answers node.","A Question node.","An HTTP request node.","A Message node."],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The HTTP request (Send HTTP request) node calls a REST API from within a topic; its response can then be parsed and used.","source":{"title":"Make HTTP requests from a topic — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-http-node"}},{"id":"gen-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A topic needs to invoke an existing agent flow to process the captured order. Which node type is designed to call an agent flow or connector action?","options":["The Generative answers node under Advanced.","A Variable management node.","A Condition node.","A Tool node."],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Tool node calls an agent flow, a connector, or another tool from within a topic.","source":{"title":"Create and edit topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-create-edit-topics"}},{"id":"gen-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A value captured in one topic must be reused across the entire agent. What should the maker use, and what should they know about changing it?","options":["A topic-scope variable, and converting it to another scope is fully reversible.","A system variable, which is read-only and can't hold captured input.","A global variable (Global. prefix); converting a topic variable to global is a one-way change.","An environment variable, which can be switched back to topic scope at any time."],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Global variables (prefixed Global.) are available agent-wide, but converting a topic variable to global is one-way and can't be reversed.","source":{"title":"Work with global variables — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-variables-bot"}},{"id":"gen-42","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker redirects from Topic A to Topic B and wants Topic B to receive the order-number variable's value. What must be configured on that variable in Topic B?","options":["Mark it to Receive values from other topics so it accepts the passed input.","Nothing — converting it to global happens automatically on redirect.","Nothing — all topic-scope variables are shared between topics by default.","Add a Parse value node in Topic A before the redirect."],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"To pass a value on a redirect, the receiving variable must be marked Receive values from other topics (input); Return values to original topics handles the reverse.","source":{"title":"Use variables — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-variables"}},{"id":"gen-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During a redirect, a maker finds that one variable can't be passed to the other topic. Which variable type is subject to this limitation?","options":["A plain text variable.","A number variable.","A boolean (true/false) variable.","A Date and time, Duration, or Multiple-choice / custom-entity variable."],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Some entity types — Date and time, Duration, Multiple-choice, and custom entities — can't be passed between topics on a redirect.","source":{"title":"Use variables — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-variables"}},{"id":"gen-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After an HTTP request returns a JSON string, a maker needs it converted into a Record so individual fields can be referenced. Which node does this?","options":["A Set value node.","A Parse value node.","A Condition node.","A Clear value node."],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Parse value node converts a JSON string into a Record, letting the topic reference the response's fields.","source":{"title":"Make HTTP requests from a topic — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-http-node"}},{"id":"gen-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A topic must follow a different path depending on whether an order was found. Which node lets the maker branch on a variable's value or a Power Fx expression?","options":["A Condition node.","A Message node.","An Adaptive Card node.","A Question node."],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Condition node branches the conversation based on a variable or a Power Fx expression.","source":{"title":"Create and edit topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-create-edit-topics"}},{"id":"gen-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to present the user with an interactive card containing buttons and input fields to collect a selection. Which node should they add?","options":["A Message node using markdown.","A Generative answers node.","An Adaptive Card node.","A Condition node."],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Adaptive Card node renders an interactive card with buttons and inputs for richer responses; a Message node only sends formatted text.","source":{"title":"Ask with an Adaptive Card — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-ask-with-adaptive-card"}},{"id":"gen-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs a reusable, authored AI call with defined inputs and outputs that multiple topics can invoke. What is this component called?","options":["A Generative answers node.","A system topic.","A global variable.","A custom prompt."],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Custom prompts are reusable authored AI calls with defined inputs and outputs that can be used inside a topic.","source":{"title":"Explore AI capabilities and prompts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/ai-capabilities"}},{"id":"gen-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A custom topic ends by redirecting to the End of Conversation system topic. What is the effect of this redirect?","options":["The conversation restarts from the Greeting topic.","The conversation ends.","Control returns to the topic that performed the redirect.","An error is raised because you can't redirect to a system topic."],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Redirecting to certain system topics — End of Conversation, Goodbye, Escalate — ends the conversation rather than returning control.","source":{"title":"Use system topics — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-system-topics"}},{"id":"gen-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is deciding how to make an agent answer HR questions from company documents rather than from the language model's own training data. Which capability should they configure to ground the agent's replies in that data?","options":["Knowledge sources, which feed generative answers (RAG)","Tools, which let the agent take actions","Agent flows, which run deterministic automation","Topics, which script fixed conversation paths"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Knowledge grounds the agent by feeding generative answers (RAG) so replies come from your data; tools act, they do not ground.","source":{"title":"Overview of knowledge in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"gen-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need an agent to ground answers on an existing Azure AI Search index. Where can Azure AI Search be added as a source?","options":["On the agent-level Knowledge page like SharePoint","Only inside a generative answers node, using the Classic data option","As a Copilot connector indexed by Microsoft Search","As a public website knowledge source"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Azure AI Search, Azure OpenAI, and custom data are only usable inside a generative-answers node via the Classic data option, not as agent-level generative knowledge.","source":{"title":"Add Azure AI Search as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"gen-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent grounded on a SharePoint site uses agent-user (signed-in user) authentication. What does this guarantee for knowledge answers?","options":["The agent caches all site content so every user gets faster answers","All users receive the same answers regardless of their permissions","The agent only surfaces content the asking user is allowed to access","Anonymous users can browse the full SharePoint library"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Agent-user authentication means retrieval is security-trimmed to each asking user's Entra ID permissions, so the agent only surfaces content that user can access.","source":{"title":"Add SharePoint as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-sharepoint"}},{"id":"gen-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A compliance team requires that the agent never answer from the model's general knowledge and only respond when a source backs the reply. Which setting achieves this?","options":["Enable Use information from the web","Turn on tenant graph grounding with semantic search","Mark every source as an Official source","Turn Allow ungrounded responses off"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"With Allow ungrounded responses off, the agent blocks any answer that did not use a knowledge source or tool and falls back instead.","source":{"title":"Generative AI settings and generative answers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-conversations"}},{"id":"gen-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An organization wants an agent to ground on enterprise content that is indexed across many back-end systems. Which knowledge source is designed for that?","options":["Copilot connectors, whose content is indexed by Microsoft Search","A public website source restricted to listed URLs","Documents uploaded to Dataverse","Azure OpenAI via the Classic data option"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot (graph) connectors index enterprise content across systems via Microsoft Search and are used as a knowledge source for grounding.","source":{"title":"Add Copilot connectors as knowledge — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-connectors"}},{"id":"gen-54","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants the agent to answer from the web but only from the company's own published sites, not the open internet. What should they configure?","options":["Enable Web Search so Bing grounds answers from anywhere","Add a public website knowledge source restricted to the company's listed URLs","Turn Allow ungrounded responses off","Connect a Dataverse table as the source"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The public website knowledge source uses Bing but is restricted to the URLs you list, unlike open web search which grounds from anywhere.","source":{"title":"Add a public website as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-public-website"}},{"id":"gen-55","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"For a SharePoint knowledge source, which authentication model does Copilot Studio apply when retrieving content?","options":["No authentication; the content is public","A shared service account with full site access","The signed-in agent user's Entra ID, with security trimming","The tenant administrator's credentials for all users"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SharePoint, Dataverse, and Copilot connectors all retrieve using the agent user's Entra ID so results are security-trimmed to that user.","source":{"title":"Add SharePoint as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-sharepoint"}},{"id":"gen-56","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker uploads PDF policy files directly to the agent as a Documents knowledge source. Where are these uploaded files stored?","options":["Azure Blob Storage","The linked SharePoint site","The Bing search index","Dataverse"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Documents knowledge source consists of files uploaded to Dataverse, which then feed the agent's generative answers.","source":{"title":"Add files as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-file-upload"}},{"id":"gen-57","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To improve SharePoint and connector retrieval, a maker wants to turn on tenant graph grounding with semantic search. What are the prerequisites?","options":["Microsoft 365 Copilot in the tenant and Authenticate with Microsoft enabled","Only a public website source added to the agent","Allow ungrounded responses turned off","A computer use tool configured on the agent"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Tenant graph grounding with semantic search improves SharePoint/connector retrieval but requires Microsoft 365 Copilot in the tenant and the Authenticate with Microsoft option.","source":{"title":"Generative AI settings and generative answers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-conversations"}},{"id":"gen-58","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent using generative orchestration has more than 25 knowledge sources. How does it decide which sources to consult for a given query?","options":["It always queries all sources in alphabetical order","It filters sources based on each source's description","It uses only the most recently created source","It ranks sources by index size"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"With generative orchestration, when there are more than 25 sources the agent filters them by their descriptions, so accurate descriptions matter.","source":{"title":"Overview of knowledge in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"gen-59","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker marks a trusted policy source as an Official source. Which statement about this setting is correct?","options":["It is available only with generative orchestration","It requires the source to use agent-user authentication","It works with classic orchestration and lets the agent use the source without verification","It automatically indexes content across all enterprise systems"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Marking a source Official (a classic-orchestration feature) tells the agent to treat it as trusted and use it without verification.","source":{"title":"Generative AI settings and generative answers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-conversations"}},{"id":"gen-60","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When knowledge is added at the agent level, which built-in element consumes it to produce grounded replies?","options":["The Fallback topic's HTTP request node","The Escalate system topic","The Start Over system topic","The Conversational boosting system topic's generative answers node"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Agent-level knowledge flows into the Conversational boosting system topic's generative answers node, which produces the grounded response.","source":{"title":"Use generative answers to boost conversations — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-conversations"}},{"id":"gen-61","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker enables Use information from the web on an agent that already has website knowledge sources. What does this setting do?","options":["Adds Bing web grounding in parallel with your website sources, in generative orchestration","Restricts answers to only the listed website URLs","Disables the Allow ungrounded responses fallback","Requires an Azure AI Search index to function"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Use information from the web (Web Search) adds Bing grounding in parallel with your own website sources and is available with generative orchestration.","source":{"title":"Generative AI settings and generative answers — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/nlu-boost-conversations"}},{"id":"gen-62","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which description correctly characterizes how the public website knowledge source retrieves content?","options":["Graph search over SharePoint site URLs","Bing search restricted to the URLs you list","Dataverse RAG over tables","Microsoft Search indexing across systems"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The public website source uses Bing but limits retrieval to the specific website URLs you provide, and requires no authentication.","source":{"title":"Add a public website as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-public-website"}},{"id":"gen-63","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During design a maker keeps confusing knowledge sources with tools. What is the essential difference?","options":["Both take actions against external systems","Both ground answers from your data","Knowledge grounds by retrieving and citing data, while tools take actions","Neither is involved in generative answers"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Knowledge grounds answers (retrieve and cite) while tools take actions; keeping them distinct is essential when designing an agent.","source":{"title":"Overview of knowledge in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"gen-64","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker adds a Dataverse table as a knowledge source so the agent can answer from case records. How does Copilot Studio retrieve from it?","options":["Bing search over the table with no authentication","Microsoft Search indexing shared across the tenant","Open public web crawl of the table data","RAG over the tables using the signed-in user's Entra ID permissions"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dataverse is used as RAG over tables and, like SharePoint and connectors, retrieves with the agent user's Entra ID so results are security-trimmed.","source":{"title":"Add Dataverse as a knowledge source — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-dataverse"}},{"id":"gen-65","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is deciding whether to add a resource to an agent as knowledge or as a tool. The agent must be able to send a confirmation email to a customer after a request is processed. Which statement correctly guides the choice?","options":["A tool should be added, because a tool lets the agent take an action such as calling a connector to send the email.","Knowledge should be added, because knowledge lets the agent perform actions like sending email.","Either one works, because knowledge and tools both execute operations on external systems.","Neither is needed, because sending email is handled automatically by classic orchestration."],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A tool lets an agent act (call an API, run a connector, operate a UI, reach an MCP server), while knowledge only grounds responses. Sending an email is an action, so it must be a tool.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-66","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to connect an agent to an existing MCP server so its tools become available. During setup you notice the option is unavailable. Which prerequisite is most likely missing?","options":["The agent must be published to at least one channel before any tool can be added.","The agent must be using generative orchestration, which is required for MCP.","A custom connector must be created first to wrap the MCP server as a REST API.","The MCP server must be re-registered as a Power Platform standard connector."],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"MCP requires generative orchestration; that mode lets the agent select tools by description and is a prerequisite for connecting an MCP server.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with the Model Context Protocol — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-mcp"}},{"id":"gen-67","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An internal billing REST API will be called by several agents across the organization. The maker wants the integration to be defined once and reused as a tool by any agent. Which approach best fits?","options":["Add an HTTP request node in a topic in each agent that needs the call.","Enable computer use so the agent drives the billing web app directly.","Build a custom connector that wraps the REST API, then add it as a tool.","Attach the API's OpenAPI file as a knowledge source on each agent."],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A custom connector wraps your own REST API as a reusable connector so it can be added as a tool by many agents, unlike a one-off HTTP node.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-68","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent must complete a task in a legacy desktop application that exposes no API, connector, or endpoint of any kind. Which tool type is designed for this situation?","options":["A prompt tool that calls a Foundry model to generate the required output.","A custom connector built from the application's documentation.","An agent flow that orchestrates the steps through Dataverse.","Computer use, which lets the agent operate the app's UI to complete the task."],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Computer use lets an agent drive a UI or browser to complete a task when no API exists to call.","source":{"title":"Automate web and desktop apps with computer use — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/computer-use"}},{"id":"gen-69","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent has several tools added. A maker wants to make sure the agent picks the correct tool when a user asks a question. What most directly determines which tool generative orchestration selects?","options":["The clarity of each tool's description, which orchestration matches against the request.","The alphabetical order of the tools on the agent's Tools page.","The date each tool was added, with the newest tool taking priority.","The number of input parameters each tool defines."],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Generative orchestration selects tools by their descriptions, so a clear, specific description is what lets the agent choose the right tool.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-70","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After connecting an agent to an MCP server, the server owner later adds two new tools and removes one. Without any change from the agent maker, what happens to the agent?","options":["The agent keeps the original tool list until the maker manually re-imports the server.","The agent's available tools sync dynamically, so the new tools appear and the removed one disappears.","The agent connection breaks and must be recreated through the onboarding wizard.","Only tools present at the first connection remain usable; later changes are ignored."],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"MCP tools and resources sync dynamically from the server, so newly added tools appear and removed ones disappear automatically.","source":{"title":"Connect to an existing MCP server — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/mcp-add-existing-server-to-agent"}},{"id":"gen-71","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs an agent to create an item in a SharePoint list using a standard, prebuilt operation rather than any custom code or endpoint. Which tool type fits best?","options":["A custom connector authored from a SharePoint OpenAPI specification.","Computer use pointed at the SharePoint web interface.","A connector action, which exposes a prebuilt Power Platform connector operation.","An MCP tool from a third-party SharePoint MCP server."],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A connector action is a prebuilt Power Platform connector operation, ideal for standard SaaS actions such as creating a SharePoint list item.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-72","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent must run a multi-step process that includes a manager approval and several conditional branches before it finishes. Which tool type is the right choice to add?","options":["A REST API tool that calls one endpoint directly.","A connector action for a single SaaS operation.","Computer use to drive the approval screens manually.","An agent flow, which provides deterministic multi-step logic including approvals."],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An agent flow provides deterministic automation for multi-step logic, approvals, and orchestration, which fits a branching approval process.","source":{"title":"Agent flows and workflows overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/flows-overview"}},{"id":"gen-73","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A tool added to an agent requires a customer ID and an order number as inputs. When a user asks the agent to check an order, how does generative orchestration supply those input values to the tool?","options":["It uses slot filling to gather the required inputs from the conversation before calling the tool.","It always prompts the maker at design time to hardcode the values.","It rejects the request unless the user provides a full JSON payload.","It copies the values from the agent's knowledge sources automatically."],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Generative orchestration selects a tool by its description and then fills the tool's inputs through slot filling, gathering the needed values from the conversation.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-74","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team connects their agent to an MCP server hosted by an external, non-Microsoft vendor. What should the team understand about responsibility for the tools they access this way?","options":["Microsoft validates and assumes responsibility for all third-party MCP tools before they can be added.","The team is responsible for the tools it accesses from a non-Microsoft server.","Responsibility transfers to the end user who triggers the tool at runtime.","No responsibility applies because MCP tools run only in a sandboxed preview."],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When connecting to an MCP server, you are responsible for the tools you access from a non-Microsoft server.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with the Model Context Protocol — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-mcp"}},{"id":"gen-75","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker connects to a single MCP server and expects it to add capability to the agent. Which statement about what an MCP server can expose is correct?","options":["An MCP server can expose exactly one tool per connection.","An MCP server exposes only knowledge sources, never actionable tools.","One MCP server can expose many tools and resources, each with its own name, description, and inputs and outputs.","An MCP server exposes tools only after they are re-authored as custom connectors."],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A single MCP server can supply many tools and resources; the server provides each tool's name, description, inputs, and outputs, which become available to the agent.","source":{"title":"Add MCP tools and resources to an agent — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/mcp-add-components-to-agent"}},{"id":"gen-76","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a reusable generative step with clearly defined inputs and outputs, backed by an AI Builder or Foundry model, that the agent can invoke as a tool. Which tool type matches this need?","options":["A connector action from a standard Power Platform connector.","Computer use targeting a generative web app.","An HTTP request node placed inside a topic.","A prompt, which is an authored AI call with defined inputs and outputs."],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A prompt is an authored AI call (AI Builder or Foundry model) used as a reusable generative step with defined I/O.","source":{"title":"Overview of prompts in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/prompts-overview"}},{"id":"gen-77","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An organization has a private internal REST API that several agents will call repeatedly over time. The maker wants the API wrapped so it behaves like any other connector-based tool. What should the maker build?","options":["A custom connector that wraps the internal REST API for reuse as a tool.","A one-off HTTP request node in a single topic.","A computer use configuration pointed at the API's Swagger page.","A child agent dedicated to holding the API definition."],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A custom connector wraps your own REST API as a reusable connector, the right choice for a private internal API called repeatedly.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with tools from a REST API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-78","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs the agent to make a single, one-off call to a public endpoint that will not be reused anywhere else and does not warrant building a connector. Which approach is most appropriate?","options":["Create a custom connector so the endpoint is registered permanently.","Use a REST API / HTTP node for a direct, one-off call to the endpoint.","Add the endpoint as an MCP resource on the agent.","Enable computer use to open the endpoint in a browser."],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A REST/HTTP node is a direct call to an endpoint, suitable for a one-off API call where no reusable connector is needed.","source":{"title":"Make HTTP requests from a topic — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-http-node"}},{"id":"gen-79","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is considering computer use for a scenario where the target system already provides a documented REST API. According to guidance, when is computer use the appropriate tool?","options":["Whenever a task involves more than one step, regardless of available APIs.","Only for reading knowledge sources that lack a connector.","When there is no API available and the agent must drive the application's UI to complete the task.","Whenever the agent uses generative orchestration, as it replaces connector actions."],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Computer use is intended for cases where no API exists, letting the agent operate the app's UI; if a REST API is available, a connector or REST tool is preferred.","source":{"title":"FAQ for computer use in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/faqs-computer-use"}},{"id":"gen-80","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent has two similarly named tools, and testing shows generative orchestration sometimes calls the wrong one. Which change is most likely to fix the selection problem?","options":["Switch the agent to classic orchestration so tool order is fixed.","Reduce the number of inputs on each tool to a single parameter.","Publish the agent to more channels so orchestration has more context.","Rewrite each tool's description to clearly and distinctly state what it does and when to use it."],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Generative orchestration chooses tools by their descriptions, so making each description clear and distinct is what resolves wrong-tool selection.","source":{"title":"Extend your agent with the Model Context Protocol — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/agent-extend-action-mcp"}},{"id":"gen-81","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker has two separate product teams that each want to own, publish, and version their own agent independently, while a central 'front-door' agent delegates to them. Which way of adding the other agents fits this requirement?","options":["Add each team's agent as a child agent so it inherits the front-door agent's model and settings","Rebuild each team's logic as topics inside the front-door agent","Add each team's agent as a connected agent so each keeps its own settings, ALM, and publishing","Add each team's agent over the A2A protocol because both agents are built in Copilot Studio"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Connected agents are other Copilot Studio agents with their own model, authentication, channels, and independent publish/ALM lifecycle, and they are reusable by multiple agents — which is what separate teams needing independent ownership require.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-82","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent has grown to roughly 50 tools, topics, and agents, and generative orchestration keeps picking the wrong one because their names and descriptions blur together. What is the recommended action?","options":["Turn off generative orchestration and route everything through classic topic triggers","Split the work into connected agents so each has a focused, distinguishable set of capabilities","Delete descriptions from the tools so orchestration relies only on names","Move all of the tools into a single child agent that shares the parent's settings"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When a main agent exceeds roughly 30-40 choices (tools, topics, and agents) and can no longer tell them apart, the guidance is to split the solution into connected agents so orchestration chooses among smaller, clearer sets.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-83","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need your Copilot Studio agent to delegate to an external agent that was built outside Microsoft's platform and exposes a standard agent interface. Which mechanism connects it?","options":["Add it as a child agent on the Agents page","Import it as a Copilot connector knowledge source","Reference it with a slash in the agent's instructions only","Connect it over the A2A (agent-to-agent) protocol"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"External, non-Microsoft agents are added over the A2A (agent-to-agent) protocol, which is the standard way to reach agents outside Copilot Studio.","source":{"title":"Connect an agent over the A2A protocol — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-agent-to-agent"}},{"id":"gen-84","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team connects a Fabric data agent to their main agent for conversational analytics, but the main agent is deployed to Microsoft 365 Copilot. What should they expect?","options":["The Fabric data agent won't work because Fabric data agents aren't supported when the main agent is deployed to M365 Copilot","The Fabric data agent works normally, with no channel-specific limitations","The Fabric data agent works only if it is added as a child agent","The Fabric data agent works but forces the main agent onto classic orchestration"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Fabric data agents don't work when the main agent is deployed to Microsoft 365 Copilot, in addition to not being redirectable from a topic node or referenceable in instructions.","source":{"title":"Consume a Fabric data agent in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-science/data-agent-microsoft-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"gen-85","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to group a handful of tools and instructions for a single task, handled by one small team, without giving that sub-agent its own model or authentication. Which option matches?","options":["A connected agent, because it is reusable across agents","A child agent, because it lives inside the parent and shares the parent's settings","An A2A external agent, because it isolates the task","A Foundry agent, because it runs a separate model"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Child agents are lightweight sub-agents inside your agent, scoped to a single use case for one small team, and they share the parent's settings and model rather than having their own.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-86","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement correctly describes how a connected agent differs from a child agent with respect to lifecycle and reuse?","options":["A connected agent cannot be published and only exists inside its parent","A connected agent shares the parent's model but can be reused by other agents","A connected agent is published together with its parent as a single unit","A connected agent is published and versioned independently and can be reused by multiple agents"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Connected agents are published and versioned independently and are reusable by multiple agents, whereas child agents are not published separately and are not reusable.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-87","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Inside a topic, you want to hand control to another agent, pass it specific input variables, and read back its output before continuing the conversation. Which wiring approach does this?","options":["Redirect to the agent from a topic node, passing inputs and reading outputs","Reference the agent in the agent's instructions with a slash","Add the agent as a knowledge source on the Knowledge page","Connect the agent over the A2A protocol so inputs flow automatically"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You can redirect to an agent from a topic node, passing inputs and reading outputs so variables flow across agents; referencing with a slash in instructions is a different, less structured wiring option.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-88","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants generative orchestration to consider another agent as an option that it can mention and delegate to directly from the agent's natural-language instructions. How is that other agent referenced?","options":["By adding it as a knowledge source and citing its name","By exporting it to a solution and importing it as an environment variable","By typing a slash in the instructions to reference the agent","By enabling it only through a redirect node in a topic"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You can reference an agent in the instructions using a slash, which lets generative orchestration delegate to it; redirecting from a topic is a separate mechanism.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-89","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A solution architect is deciding whether to split one agent into several collaborating agents. Which trade-off should they weigh against the benefits of specialization?","options":["Multi-agent solutions cannot use generative orchestration at all","Connected agents lose their own authentication once linked","Child agents must each be published independently, increasing overhead","Extra orchestration hops add latency and enlarge the testing and governance surface"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The main trade-offs of multi-agent orchestration are added latency from extra orchestration hops and a larger testing and governance surface.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-90","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After a maker adds a child agent, a connected agent, and an external A2A agent, where can they see all of these listed together for the agent?","options":["On the agent's Agents page, where all added agents appear","Only in the Power Platform admin center's connector list","On the Knowledge page alongside data sources","Only in the published solution's environment variables"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"All added agents — child, connected, and external — appear on the agent's Agents page.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-91","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker asks whether a single agent can use both a lightweight in-agent sub-agent for one task and a separately owned agent for a reusable capability at the same time. What is true?","options":["No, an agent must use either child agents or connected agents, not both","No, mixing requires converting the child agent into a connected agent first","Yes, you can mix child and connected agents in the same solution","Yes, but only if both are connected over the A2A protocol"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You can mix child and connected agents in the same agent, combining lightweight in-agent sub-agents with independently owned, reusable connected agents.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-92","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to delegate to a Microsoft Foundry agent from Copilot Studio. Which statement about connecting Foundry agents is accurate?","options":["Foundry agents are added as knowledge sources rather than agents","Foundry agents connect as a connected/external agent and are currently in preview","Foundry agents are child agents that share the parent's model","Foundry agents require the A2A protocol and are generally available"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Microsoft Foundry agents (in preview) connect as a connected/external agent so your agent can delegate to them.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Microsoft Foundry agent — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"gen-93","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker connects a Fabric data agent and then tries to hand off to it from a topic node using a redirect, and also to name it in the agent's instructions. What will they find?","options":["Both work, but only when the agent is deployed to M365 Copilot","Both work exactly as they do for connected Copilot Studio agents","Only the redirect works; instruction references are unsupported for it","Neither works: a Fabric data agent can't be redirected to from a topic node or referenced in instructions"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Fabric data agents can't be redirected to from a topic node and can't be referenced in instructions, and they also don't work when the main agent is deployed to Microsoft 365 Copilot.","source":{"title":"Consume a Fabric data agent in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-science/data-agent-microsoft-copilot-studio"}},{"id":"gen-94","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During testing of a multi-agent solution, a maker wants to confirm which agent actually handled each turn of a conversation. Where can they see this?","options":["In the activity map, which shows which agent handled each turn","In the environment variable values of the published solution","In the Knowledge page's source usage report","Only by enabling Application Insights and querying logs"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When testing, the activity map shows which agent handled each turn, making the delegation across agents visible.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-95","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A single team wants to keep one agent but group a set of tools and instructions for a well-defined task so orchestration handles that task as a unit, without needing separate publishing. Which choice best fits?","options":["A connected agent, to publish the task independently","An external A2A agent, to isolate the task's governance","A child agent, to group the task inside the same agent","A Fabric data agent, to keep the task conversational"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A child agent is the lightweight, in-agent way to group tools and instructions for a single task within one team, without separate publishing.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-96","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Two independent teams each maintain their own solution and release cadence, and one team's agent must be callable by several other agents across the environment. Which characteristic of a connected agent makes it the right fit?","options":["It always shares the calling agent's authentication and channels","It is a separate, independently published agent with its own settings that is reusable across agents","It exists only inside the parent agent and cannot be reused","It can only be reached over the A2A protocol"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Connected agents suit separate teams and independent solutions because they have their own model, authentication, and channels, are published independently, and are reusable by multiple agents.","source":{"title":"Multi-agent orchestration patterns — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/multi-agent-patterns"}},{"id":"gen-97","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An enterprise already serves its policy documents from an existing Azure AI Search index. A maker wants the Copilot Studio agent to ground answers on that index. Where must the Azure AI Search connection be configured?","options":["Inside a generative answers node, using the Classic data option","At the agent level, as a top-level knowledge source with generative orchestration","In the agent's authentication settings under manual connection","As an Application Insights telemetry destination"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Azure AI Search (and Azure OpenAI) can be added as a knowledge source only inside a generative-answers node via the Classic data option, not as an agent-level generative-orchestration source.","source":{"title":"Add an Azure AI Search knowledge source — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"gen-98","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker enabled generative orchestration on an agent and now expects to attach an Azure OpenAI resource directly at the agent level as a knowledge source. Why does this approach fail?","options":["Azure OpenAI can only be attached to a Power Automate flow, never to an agent","Azure AI Search and Azure OpenAI are supported only within a generative-answers node using the Classic data option, not at the agent level","Azure OpenAI requires the agent to be published to Microsoft Teams first","Generative orchestration disables all knowledge sources of any kind"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Azure AI Search and Azure OpenAI grounding is available only through the Classic data option inside a generative-answers node; they cannot be added as agent-level knowledge with generative orchestration.","source":{"title":"Add an Azure AI Search knowledge source — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"gen-99","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is building a custom prompt (prompt tool) and wants to control which underlying model generates the response so they can balance quality against cost. Which Azure capability lets them make that choice?","options":["The Application Insights telemetry pipeline","The Azure AI Search semantic ranker","The Microsoft Foundry model catalog","The Power Platform data loss prevention policy"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Foundry model catalog lets a maker pick the specific model that powers a custom prompt, choosing one suited to the task and cost.","source":{"title":"Bring your own model for prompts — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/bring-your-own-model-prompts"}},{"id":"gen-100","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An operations team wants dashboards and alerts that track an agent's conversations, events, errors, and performance in Azure. Which service should the agent send its telemetry to?","options":["Azure AI Search","Microsoft Foundry model catalog","Microsoft Dataverse audit log","Azure Monitor Application Insights"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Agent telemetry (conversations, events, errors, performance) is streamed to Azure Monitor Application Insights for dashboards and alerting.","source":{"title":"Monitor AI agents with Application Insights — Azure Monitor","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/app/agents-view"}},{"id":"gen-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Before an agent can stream telemetry to Application Insights, an administrator finds the capability is blocked. Which Power Platform control most likely governs whether Application Insights is allowed?","options":["A data policy (DLP) in the Power Platform admin center","The agent's end-user authentication mode","The Foundry model catalog access tier","The publishing channel configuration"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Enabling Application Insights telemetry is governed by data policies (DLP) in the Power Platform admin center; a policy can allow or block that capability.","source":{"title":"Capture telemetry with Application Insights — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/advanced-bot-framework-composer-capture-telemetry"}},{"id":"gen-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants their Copilot Studio agent to delegate certain requests to an existing agent hosted in Microsoft Foundry. How does the Foundry agent join the solution?","options":["It is imported as a Classic data knowledge source in a generative-answers node","It is added as a connected (external) agent that the orchestrator can delegate to","It replaces the agent's default model in the Foundry model catalog","It is registered only as an Application Insights telemetry source"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Foundry agent plugs in as a connected/external agent in a multi-agent setup, so the primary agent can delegate to it.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Foundry agent — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"gen-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs to ground responses on an Azure OpenAI resource. In the authoring canvas, which node and option expose Azure OpenAI as a supported knowledge source?","options":["The HTTP request node, under custom connectors","The agent-level knowledge pane, under generative orchestration","A generative answers node, under the Classic data option","The topic trigger node, under adaptive cards"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Like Azure AI Search, Azure OpenAI is added only inside a generative-answers node through the Classic data option.","source":{"title":"Add an Azure AI Search knowledge source — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"gen-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants grounded generative answers that combine a specific Foundry-hosted model with their enterprise Azure AI Search index. Which combination achieves this?","options":["Application Insights telemetry paired with a DLP policy","A Power Platform connector paired with an adaptive card","A Foundry agent paired with a computer use tool","A Foundry model catalog model paired with an Azure AI Search index in a generative-answers node"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Grounded generative answers can combine a Foundry-hosted model with an Azure AI Search index (via the Classic data option) to produce grounded responses.","source":{"title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/retrieval-augmented-generation"}},{"id":"gen-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An analyst wants to understand exactly what data flows to Application Insights when it is enabled for an agent. Which set of data does the agent emit as telemetry?","options":["Conversations, events, errors, and performance","Only the agent's published channel list","The Foundry model catalog pricing tiers","The DLP policy definitions applied to the environment"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The agent sends telemetry such as conversations, events, errors, and performance to Application Insights.","source":{"title":"Capture telemetry with Application Insights — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/advanced-bot-framework-composer-capture-telemetry"}},{"id":"gen-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Two custom prompts in an agent have different needs: one summarizes long legal text and one classifies short support tickets cheaply. What does the Foundry model catalog let the maker do to serve both?","options":["Route each prompt's telemetry to a separate Application Insights resource","Choose a model suited to each prompt's task and cost profile","Attach a distinct Azure AI Search index to each prompt automatically","Publish each prompt to a different channel"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Foundry model catalog lets a maker pick, per custom prompt, a model suited to that task and its cost requirements.","source":{"title":"Overview of prompts — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/prompts-overview"}},{"id":"gen-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A governance lead needs to confirm which capability determines whether makers in an environment may turn on Application Insights for their agents. Where is that decision enforced?","options":["In the generative answers node's Classic data option","In the Foundry model catalog per-model settings","In a data policy (DLP) configured in the Power Platform admin center","In the agent's test-panel activity map"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Application Insights is one of the capabilities a data policy (DLP) in the Power Platform admin center can allow or block.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is designing a multi-agent solution where a Copilot Studio agent routes specialized pricing questions to a Foundry-hosted agent. Which pattern correctly describes how the Foundry agent participates?","options":["It is embedded as a generative-answers Classic data source","It becomes the agent's default catalog model for all prompts","It is added only as an Application Insights alert rule","It is connected as an external agent that the orchestrator delegates work to"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In a multi-agent design, a Foundry agent is connected as an external/connected agent so the primary agent can delegate matching requests to it.","source":{"title":"Connect to a Foundry agent — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/add-agent-foundry-agent"}},{"id":"gen-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker adds a generative answers node and is looking for the setting that lets them wire in Azure AI Search. Which option within the node exposes that Azure grounding source?","options":["The Classic data option","The agent-level generative orchestration toggle","The adaptive card designer","The end-user authentication tab"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Within a generative-answers node, the Classic data option is where Azure AI Search (or Azure OpenAI) is connected as a grounding source.","source":{"title":"Add an Azure AI Search knowledge source — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-azure-ai-search"}},{"id":"gen-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A company already maintains a curated Azure AI Search index over thousands of product manuals and wants retrieval-augmented answers without re-uploading files into Copilot Studio. What is the recommended approach?","options":["Re-upload every manual as an in-product file knowledge source","Connect the existing Azure AI Search index in a generative-answers node's Classic data option","Register the manuals as a REST API tool","Stream the manuals through Application Insights telemetry"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"For enterprise document indexes already served by Azure AI Search, connect that existing index in the generative-answers node (Classic data) rather than re-importing the documents.","source":{"title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/retrieval-augmented-generation"}},{"id":"gen-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants a custom prompt to run on a particular model from Azure rather than the default. Which tool and Azure resource pairing accomplishes this?","options":["A generative-answers node paired with Application Insights","A REST API tool paired with a DLP policy","A prompt tool (custom prompt) paired with a model from the Foundry model catalog","A connected agent paired with an Azure AI Search index"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A custom prompt (prompt tool) is where you choose the underlying model, selecting it from the Foundry model catalog.","source":{"title":"Bring your own model for prompts — Microsoft Copilot Studio","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/bring-your-own-model-prompts"}},{"id":"gen-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After enabling Application Insights for an agent, where will operators go to build dashboards and configure alerts on the agent's activity?","options":["The Copilot Studio test panel activity map","The Power Platform data policy editor","The Foundry model catalog","Azure Monitor, where the Application Insights telemetry lands"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.4","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Telemetry sent to Application Insights surfaces in Azure Monitor, where operators build dashboards and set up alerting on agent activity.","source":{"title":"Monitor AI agents with Application Insights — Azure Monitor","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/app/agents-view"}},{"id":"gen-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"While authoring an agent, a maker wants to see which topic, tool, or connected agent was chosen at each turn of a test conversation so they can confirm the routing is correct. Which feature shows this?","options":["The CSAT survey results page","The published channel's transcript export","The activity map in the Test agent panel","The Power Platform admin center capacity report"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Test agent panel checks conversation flow while authoring, and its activity map shows which topic, tool, or agent was selected on each turn so you can verify routing.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is worried that repeatedly using the Test agent panel during development will eat into their organization's Copilot Studio message capacity. What should you tell them?","options":["Test runs in the authoring canvas do not consume capacity","Each test conversation consumes one billed message","Testing consumes capacity only after the agent is published","Testing consumes double capacity because it simulates users"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Testing an agent while authoring does not consume Copilot Studio capacity, so makers can iterate freely in the Test agent panel.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-115","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to evaluate their HR agent the same way across every release instead of relying on whoever happens to be chatting in the test panel that day. What should they create?","options":["A single ad-hoc question in the Test agent panel","A separate managed solution for each release","A new environment variable per release","A test set of representative inputs with expected outcomes"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A test set is a curated collection of representative inputs and expected outcomes used to evaluate the agent consistently and repeatably, which is more reliable than ad-hoc test-panel chatting.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-116","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In Copilot Studio agent evaluation, what best describes a single test case?","options":["A published channel the agent is deployed to","One interaction that simulates a user turn and can include the expected response","A pipeline stage that promotes the agent to production","A capacity report for a given environment"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A test case is a single interaction that simulates how a user interacts with the agent and can include the expected response; a group of test cases forms a test set.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-117","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker needs to build a test set quickly for an agent that already has knowledge sources and topics. Which approach is supported in Copilot Studio?","options":["Generate cases from knowledge or topics, import a test set file, or write cases manually","Only record a live production conversation and replay it","Only import test cases from Azure DevOps work items","Only write every test case by hand in a spreadsheet"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio lets you generate test cases from knowledge or topics, import a test set file, or write cases manually, so you are not limited to a single method.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-118","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After running an evaluation with a test set, an author opens the results to diagnose failures. Beyond a simple pass/fail, which of these is the most useful thing to inspect for a wrong answer?","options":["The number of channels the agent is published to","The environment's total storage consumption","Whether the agent selected the right tool or topic and grounded its answer","The managed-solution version number"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Reviewing evaluation results means checking whether the agent picked the right tool or topic, grounded and cited the answer, produced the correct result, and how it performed on latency.","source":{"title":"Run evaluations and view results — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-results"}},{"id":"gen-119","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When Copilot Studio finishes running an evaluation, how are the results scored?","options":["The whole test set receives a single letter grade","Each test case is marked Pass, Fail, Invalid, or Error, and the set gets a pass rate","Only total latency is reported, with no per-case outcome","The set is compared against the exam's 700 passing score"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio simulates the conversations and assigns each test case a Pass, Fail, Invalid, or Error, then reports a pass rate for the test set under recent results.","source":{"title":"Run evaluations and view results — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-results"}},{"id":"gen-120","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An evaluation shows several test cases failing because the agent gave answers that were not supported by any knowledge source. According to the evaluation checklist approach, what is the right next step?","options":["Immediately republish the agent to a new channel","Increase the environment's message capacity","Delete the failing test cases so the pass rate rises","Do root-cause analysis and improve instructions, descriptions, or knowledge, then re-run"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The evaluation checklist guides you to find the root cause of failures such as ungrounded answers, then improve descriptions, instructions, knowledge, or tools and re-run the evaluation to confirm the score improves.","source":{"title":"Review the agent evaluation checklist — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agents/agent-evaluation/evaluation-checklist"}},{"id":"gen-121","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to run the same agent evaluation automatically as part of a CI pipeline instead of clicking Run in the maker portal each time. What should they use?","options":["The Power Platform API to automate agent evaluations","The Test agent panel's activity map","The end-of-conversation CSAT survey","A manual export of the transcript to CSV"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Copilot Studio evaluations can be automated with the Power Platform API, letting teams run test sets programmatically, for example inside a pipeline, rather than only in the portal.","source":{"title":"Automate agent evaluations with the Power Platform API — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-rest-api"}},{"id":"gen-122","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After go-live, a product owner wants to know what percentage of user questions the agent actually answered with a generated response. Which analytics metric answers this?","options":["The managed-solution import log","The test set pass rate","The generated answer rate","The number of environment variables in the solution"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The generated answer rate is an ongoing analytics metric that reports the share of questions the agent answered with a generated response, helping you spot coverage gaps after publishing.","source":{"title":"Analytics overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-overview"}},{"id":"gen-123","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker wants to discover the most common clusters of user intents hitting the live agent so they can prioritize which topics to build next. Which analytics feature surfaces this?","options":["The activity map","Themes, which cluster user intents from real conversations","The connection references list","The test case Error count"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The themes analytics feature clusters real user intents into groups, helping makers find unanswered or high-volume themes to drive the next round of improvements.","source":{"title":"Analytics overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-overview"}},{"id":"gen-124","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Leadership asks how satisfied end users are with the published agent. Which metric most directly captures user satisfaction?","options":["The generated answer rate","The evaluation test set pass rate","The number of knowledge sources attached","CSAT, derived from the end-of-conversation survey"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"CSAT is captured from the end-of-conversation survey and is the analytics metric that most directly reflects user satisfaction with the agent.","source":{"title":"Analytics overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-overview"}},{"id":"gen-125","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent has three knowledge sources attached, but the team suspects one is rarely helping answer questions. Which analytics view helps them confirm this before removing it?","options":["Knowledge source use analytics","The Power Platform pipeline run history","The Test agent panel activity map only","The environment's audit log"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Knowledge source use analytics shows which sources are actually being used to ground answers, so the team can identify low-value sources and decide whether to keep or replace them.","source":{"title":"Analytics overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-overview"}},{"id":"gen-126","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker distinguishes between validating an agent before shipping and monitoring it after go-live. Which pairing correctly matches the activity to the tool?","options":["Pre-ship validation uses CSAT; post-ship monitoring uses a test set","Both pre-ship and post-ship rely solely on the activity map","Pre-ship validation uses a test set evaluation; post-ship monitoring uses ongoing analytics","Pre-ship validation uses environment variables; post-ship monitoring uses connection references"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Before shipping you validate with a test set evaluation; after publishing you keep watching ongoing analytics such as answer rate, source use, themes, and CSAT to track live health.","source":{"title":"About agent evaluation — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-intro"}},{"id":"gen-127","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An evaluation reveals the agent frequently calls the wrong tool for a given question. Which change is the most appropriate first fix to improve tool selection?","options":["Add more channels so the agent has more traffic","Refine the tool and topic descriptions and instructions, then re-run the evaluation","Delete the test set and rely on manual chatting","Increase the environment's storage quota"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Wrong tool selection is usually addressed by improving the descriptions and instructions the model uses to route, then re-running the evaluation to confirm the score improves.","source":{"title":"Review the agent evaluation checklist — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agents/agent-evaluation/evaluation-checklist"}},{"id":"gen-128","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During evaluation review, an author notices some responses are correct and grounded but take noticeably long to return. Which quality dimension does this finding relate to?","options":["Grounding and citation accuracy","Managed versus unmanaged solution state","Channel publication scope","Latency of the agent's responses"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When reviewing evaluation results you inspect not only tool selection, grounding, and correctness but also latency, which measures how quickly the agent responds.","source":{"title":"Run evaluations and view results — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/analytics-agent-evaluation-results"}},{"id":"gen-129","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is building an HR agent in the development environment and wants to keep editing its topics and flows as they iterate. Which type of solution should the agent be authored in at this stage?","options":["A managed solution, so components are locked during authoring","A default solution that cannot be exported","An unmanaged solution, where components remain editable","A patch solution layered on top of a managed base"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"You author in an unmanaged solution in dev, where components are editable; managed solutions are locked and used for test/prod.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-130","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An organization deploys an agent to the production environment and later needs to remove it completely, leaving no orphaned components behind. Which solution type provides a clean uninstall?","options":["An unmanaged solution, because deleting it also deletes all its components","A managed solution, which supports a clean uninstall","A default solution, which is removed automatically at import","Either type, since removal behavior is identical"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Managed solutions can be cleanly uninstalled, removing their components; with unmanaged solutions components stay behind even if the solution is deleted.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-131","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During a deployment review, a developer must explain what happens to individual components when an unmanaged solution is deleted from an environment. Which statement is correct?","options":["The components remain in the environment even after the solution is deleted","All components are automatically removed with the solution","The components become read-only but stay installed","The environment blocks deletion until the solution is exported"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In an unmanaged solution, deleting the solution leaves its components in place; only managed solutions cleanly remove components on uninstall.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-132","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent calls an internal API whose base URL is different in dev, test, and production. The team wants to promote the same solution without editing the agent in each environment. What should they use to hold the URL?","options":["A hard-coded value baked into the topic","A separate managed solution per environment","A connection reference bound to the API URL","An environment variable that supplies the URL per environment"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Environment variables externalize values that differ per environment (like URLs and IDs) so you change configuration without editing the agent.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-133","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After importing an agent solution to production, a connector must use production credentials rather than the dev account it was built with. Which solution component lets the connector rebind to the correct credentials in each environment?","options":["An environment variable pointing at the credential","A managed solution patch","A connection reference for the connector","A Power Platform pipeline stage"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A connection reference abstracts a connector's connection so it rebinds to the right credentials in each environment.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-134","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to promote an agent solution from development to test to production using an in-product capability rather than manual steps. Which Power Platform feature is designed for this?","options":["Power Platform Pipelines","Azure DevOps release gates only","Fabric deployment pipelines","The solution checker"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Platform Pipelines promote solutions dev to test to prod inside the product; it is the Power Platform equivalent of Fabric deployment pipelines.","source":{"title":"Application lifecycle management (ALM) with Power Platform — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/alm/overview-alm"}},{"id":"gen-135","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team is not using Power Platform Pipelines and needs another way to move a finished agent from dev to production. Which manual approach is a valid alternative?","options":["Copy the agent's topics into a new agent by hand in prod","Export the managed solution from dev and import it to test and prod","Share the dev agent directly with production users","Recreate all connection references from scratch in prod only"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"As an alternative to pipelines, you can export the managed solution from dev and import it to test/prod manually.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-136","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An ALM lead wants an auditable history of changes to an agent, with the ability to deploy from a source repository. Which capability supports this?","options":["Storing exported solution zips on a file share","Enabling the solution checker on every save","Turning the agent into an unmanaged layer","Git-backed source control with deploy-from-Git for the agent"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Agents support Git-backed source control and deploy-from-Git, giving an auditable change history.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-137","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker is packaging an agent for deployment and wants to ensure that its topics, agent flows, custom connectors, connection references, and environment variables all travel together. What is the recommended approach?","options":["Put the agent in one solution and each dependency in its own separate solution","Deploy the agent first, then add dependencies directly in prod","Add the agent and its dependencies to one solution","Rely on the default solution to include dependencies automatically"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The agent and its dependencies (topics, agent flows, custom connectors, connection references, environment variables) should be added to one solution so they deploy together.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-138","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An agent needs an API key that must not be hard-coded and should differ per environment, with the secret stored securely. How should this be handled in the solution?","options":["Use an environment variable that references the key (for example via Key Vault)","Embed the key directly in the topic and export it with the solution","Add the key as a connection reference display name","Store the key in the agent's description field"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Environment variables externalize values such as keys (secured via Key Vault) so configuration changes per environment without editing the agent.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-139","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A developer imports a managed agent solution into the test environment and then tries to edit one of its topics directly. What is the expected behavior?","options":["The topic is editable because managed solutions unlock in test","The whole solution reverts to unmanaged on first edit","The topic can be edited but changes are discarded on next import","The components are locked because managed solutions are not editable"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Managed solutions are locked and not editable; you author in unmanaged (dev) and deploy managed to test/prod.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-140","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants a single sentence to guide their agent ALM strategy about where each solution type belongs. Which statement is correct?","options":["Author in a managed solution in dev; deploy an unmanaged solution to prod","Author in an unmanaged solution in dev; deploy a managed solution to test and prod","Use unmanaged solutions in every environment for flexibility","Use managed solutions in every environment including dev"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The golden rule is to author in an unmanaged solution (dev) and deploy a managed solution (locked, cleanly uninstallable) to test/prod.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-141","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After the managed agent solution is promoted to production, the environment variables and connection references still point at dev resources. What is the correct final step?","options":["Re-export the solution from dev with prod values baked in","Convert the prod solution back to unmanaged to edit values","In prod, set the environment variable and connection values for that environment","Delete and recreate the agent directly in production"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"After promotion, you set the environment variable and connection values in prod so production points at production resources.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}},{"id":"gen-142","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A maker asks which components should be included when adding an agent to a solution so nothing breaks after import. Which set is correct?","options":["Topics, agent flows, custom connectors, connection references, and environment variables","Only the agent shell, since dependencies are resolved automatically","Only the agent and its analytics dashboards","The agent plus the target environment's security roles only"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Add the agent along with its dependencies: topics, agent flows, custom connectors, connection references, and environment variables.","source":{"title":"Create and manage solutions in Copilot Studio — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/authoring-solutions-overview"}},{"id":"gen-143","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer familiar with Fabric deployment pipelines asks what the equivalent in-product promotion tool is for agent solutions in Power Platform. What is the correct answer?","options":["The Power Platform admin center backup feature","Power Platform Pipelines","Azure Resource Manager templates","The Copilot Studio publish button"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Platform Pipelines are the Power Platform equivalent of Fabric deployment pipelines, promoting solutions dev to test to prod in-product.","source":{"title":"Deployment failure mitigation strategy — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/well-architected/operational-excellence/mitigation-strategy"}},{"id":"gen-144","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants config that can travel across environments without editing the agent itself. Which pairing of ALM pieces achieves this?","options":["Managed solutions and the solution checker","Patch solutions and default solutions","Publishing and sharing the agent","Environment variables and connection references"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Environment variables plus connection references provide configuration that travels across environments without editing the agent.","source":{"title":"Establish an ALM strategy for agents — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/guidance/alm"}}];
